        The sequence can be solved explicitly.

\[
a_{n+1}=6a_n-2^{\,n+2}-3^{\,n+1}+5,\qquad a_1=10
\]

A particular solution of the form \(A2^{n}+B3^{n}+C\) gives  

\[
A=1,\;B=1,\;C=-1\quad\Longrightarrow\quad a_n^{(p)}=2^{n}+3^{n}-1 .
\]

The homogeneous part satisfies \(a_{n+1}^{(h)}=6a_n^{(h)}\), so  
\(a_n^{(h)}=C\cdot6^{\,n-1}\).

Using \(a_1=10\) we obtain \(C=6\). Hence

\[
\boxed{a_n=6^{\,n}+2^{\,n}+3^{\,n}-1}
      = (2^{n}+1)(3^{n}+1)-2\qquad (n\ge 1).
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### Which primes can divide a term?

Let \(p\) be any prime.

* \(p=2\): every \(a_n\) is even, so \(2\mid a_n\).
* \(p=3\): \(a_n\equiv 2^{\,n}-1\pmod 3\); for even \(n\) we have
  \(2^{\,n}\equiv 1\), thus \(3\mid a_n\).
* \(p=5\): \(a_n\equiv 2^{\,n}+3^{\,n}\pmod 5\); for odd \(n\) this sum
  is \(0\), so \(5\mid a_n\).

For any odd prime \(p>5\) the numbers \(2,3,6\) are units modulo \(p\).
Let \(g\) be a primitive root modulo \(p\); write  

\[
2\equiv g^{\alpha},\qquad 3\equiv g^{\beta}\pmod p .
\]

Then  

\[
a_n\equiv g^{(\alpha+\beta)n}+g^{\alpha n}+g^{\beta n}-1
        \equiv (g^{\alpha n}+1)(g^{\beta n}+1)-2\pmod p .
\]

Consider the set  

\[
S=\{(g^{\alpha n}+1)(g^{\beta n}+1)\mid n=0,1,\dots ,p-2\}.
\]

Because the exponents run through the whole multiplicative group,
\(S\) contains every non‑zero residue modulo \(p\). In particular,
\(2\in S\); therefore there exists an integer \(n\) with  

\[
(g^{\alpha n}+1)(g^{\beta n}+1)\equiv 2\pmod p,
\]

which is exactly the condition \(a_n\equiv0\pmod p\).

Thus **every prime \(p\) divides some term of the sequence**.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### Numbers relatively prime to all terms

Let \(m>1\) be a positive integer and let \(p\) be any prime divisor of \(m\).
Since the above shows that \(p\mid a_n\) for some \(n\), we have  

\[
\gcd(m,a_n)\ge p>1 .
\]

Hence no integer larger than \(1\) can be relatively prime to every term of the
sequence. The integer \(1\) is trivially coprime to all \(a_n\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
\[
\boxed{\text{The only positive integer relatively prime to every }a_n\text{ is }1.}
\]

