### 1.  Closed form of the sequence  

The recurrence is linear with a non‑homogeneous term that is a linear combination of the
geometric sequences \(2^{n}\) and \(3^{n}\).  
A particular solution of the form \(p_{n}= \alpha 2^{n}+\beta 3^{n}+\gamma\) gives  

\[
\alpha =\beta =1,\qquad \gamma =-1 .
\]

Hence  

\[
a_{n}=C\cdot 6^{\,n-1}+2^{n}+3^{n}-1 .
\]

Using \(a_{1}=10\) we obtain \(C=6\). Therefore  

\[
\boxed{\,a_{n}=6^{\,n}+2^{n}+3^{n}-1\;}\qquad (n\ge 1).
\]

---

### 2.  Every prime divides some term  

Let \(p\) be a prime.  
If \(p=2\) or \(p=3\) we have \(2\mid a_{2}=48\) and \(3\mid a_{2}=48\).

Assume \(p>3\).  
Since \(p\not\mid 2,3,6\), the numbers \(2,3,6\) are units modulo \(p\) and each
has a multiplicative order dividing \(p-1\).  
Consequently the sequence \(\{a_{n}\}_{n\ge 0}\) is periodic modulo \(p\) with period
\(L\mid(p-1)\); in particular the values

\[
a_{0},a_{1},\dots ,a_{L-1}\qquad\bigl(a_{0}=6^{0}+2^{0}+3^{0}-1=2\bigr)
\]

run through all residues of the form  

\[
6^{n}+2^{n}+3^{n}-1\pmod p\qquad (0\le n<L).
\]

Consider the sum of these \(L\) residues.  Using the geometric‑series formula and the
fact that \(2^{L}\equiv3^{L}\equiv6^{L}\equiv1\pmod p\),

\[
\sum_{n=0}^{L-1}a_{n}
 =\sum_{n=0}^{L-1}(6^{n}+2^{n}+3^{n}-1)
 \equiv 0+0+0-L\equiv 0\pmod p .
\]

Thus the sum of the \(L\) residues is \(0\) modulo \(p\).
If none of the residues were \(0\) modulo \(p\), each would be a non‑zero element of the
field \(\mathbb{F}_{p}\) and the sum of \(L\) non‑zero elements could not be \(0\) because
\(L\le p-1\) and the only way a sum of at most \(p-1\) non‑zero field elements can be
\(0\) is that at least one of them is \(0\).  Hence some index \(n\) satisfies  

\[
a_{n}\equiv0\pmod p .
\]

Therefore **every prime \(p\) divides at least one term of the sequence**.

---

### 3.  Numbers coprime to all terms  

Let \(m>1\) be an integer and let \(q\) be any prime divisor of \(m\).
From the previous part there exists \(n\) with \(q\mid a_{n}\); consequently
\(\gcd(m,a_{n})\ge q>1\).  
Hence no integer \(m>1\) can be relatively prime to every term of the sequence.

The only positive integer that is coprime to all the \(a_{n}\) is \(1\).

\[
\boxed{\,1\,}
\]

