### 1.  Closed form of the sequence  

Write the recurrence as  

\[
a_{n+1}=6a_{n}-4\cdot2^{n}-3\cdot3^{n}+5 .
\]

Look for a particular solution of the shape \(A2^{n}+B3^{n}+C\).  
Substituting gives  

\[
A2^{n+1}+B3^{n+1}+C=6(A2^{n}+B3^{n}+C)-4\cdot2^{n}-3\cdot3^{n}+5 .
\]

Equating the coefficients of \(2^{n},3^{n}\) and the constant term yields  

\[
2A=6A-4\;\Longrightarrow\;A=1,\qquad 
3B=6B-3\;\Longrightarrow\;B=1,\qquad 
C=6C+5\;\Longrightarrow\;C=-1 .
\]

Hence a particular solution is \(2^{n}+3^{n}-1\).  
The homogeneous part satisfies \(h_{n+1}=6h_{n}\), i.e. \(h_{n}=C\cdot6^{\,n-1}\).

Using \(a_{1}=10\) we obtain \(C=6\).  
Therefore  

\[
\boxed{\,a_{n}=6^{\,n}+2^{\,n}+3^{\,n}-1\qquad (n\ge 1)\,}.
\]

---

### 2.  Which primes divide a term?

*For \(p=2\):*  
\(6^{n},2^{n}\) are even and \(3^{n}\) is odd, so \(a_{n}= \text{even}\) for every \(n\).  
Thus \(2\mid a_{n}\) for all \(n\).

*For \(p=3\):*  
\(a_{2}=6^{2}+2^{2}+3^{2}-1=48\) is a multiple of \(3\); indeed \(3\mid a_{n}\) whenever \(n\) is even.

*For any odd prime \(p>3\):*  

Because \(p\) does not divide \(2,3,6\), Fermat’s little theorem gives  

\[
2^{p-2}\equiv 2^{-1},\qquad 
3^{p-2}\equiv 3^{-1},\qquad 
6^{p-2}\equiv 6^{-1}\pmod p .
\]

Take \(n=p-2\). Then  

\[
\begin{aligned}
a_{p-2}&=6^{p-2}+2^{p-2}+3^{p-2}-1 \\
&\equiv 6^{-1}+2^{-1}+3^{-1}-1\pmod p .
\end{aligned}
\]

Multiply the congruence by the invertible number \(6\) (mod \(p\)):

\[
6\bigl(6^{-1}+2^{-1}+3^{-1}-1\bigr)\equiv
1+3+2-6\equiv0\pmod p .
\]

Since \(6\) is invertible, the bracket itself is \(0\pmod p\); hence  

\[
p\mid a_{p-2}.
\]

Thus **every odd prime \(p\) divides some term of the sequence** (for \(p=3\) the divisor is \(a_{2}\), for \(p>3\) the divisor is \(a_{p-2}\)).

---

### 3.  Numbers coprime to all terms

Let \(m>1\) be a positive integer and let \(p\) be any prime divisor of \(m\).  
From the previous step, there exists an index \(n\) such that \(p\mid a_{n}\).  
Consequently  

\[
\gcd(m,a_{n})\ge p>1,
\]

so \(m\) cannot be relatively prime to every term of the sequence.

The only positive integer that has no prime divisor is \(1\), and clearly \(\gcd(1,a_{n})=1\) for all \(n\).

---

\[
\boxed{\,1\,}
\]

Hence the set of positive numbers relatively prime to every element of the sequence \((a_n)\) consists solely of the number \(1\).

