        [Turn 9 REASONING] (2509 chars)
        \[
a_{1}=10,\qquad a_{n+1}=6a_{n}-2^{\,n+2}-3^{\,n+1}+5\;(n\ge 1)
\]

---

### 1.  Closed form of the sequence  

Write the recurrence as  

\[
a_{n+1}=6a_{n}-2^{n+2}-3^{n+1}+5 .
\]

A particular solution of the form \(A\cdot2^{n}+B\cdot3^{n}+C\) gives  

\[
A=1,\;B=1,\;C=-1 .
\]

The homogeneous part is \(C\cdot6^{\,n-1}\).  
Using \(a_{1}=10\) we obtain \(C=6\).  
Hence for every \(n\ge 1\)

\[
\boxed{a_{n}=6^{\,n}+2^{\,n}+3^{\,n}-1 } .
\]

Equivalently  

\[
a_{n}+2=(2^{\,n}+1)(3^{\,n}+1). \tag{1}
\]

---

### 2.  Every prime divides some term  

*For \(p=2\) and \(p=3\).*  
From (1) we have \(a_{n}\equiv0\pmod 2\) for all \(n\) and
\(a_{2k}\equiv0\pmod 3\); thus \(2,3\mid a_{n}\) for suitable \(n\).

*For a prime \(p>3\).*  
Both \(2\) and \(3\) are invertible modulo \(p\).  
Let  

\[
\ell_{2}= \operatorname{ord}_{p}(2),\qquad 
\ell_{3}= \operatorname{ord}_{p}(3),\qquad 
L=\operatorname{lcm}(\ell_{2},\ell_{3}).
\]

Then \(2^{\,n+L}\equiv2^{\,n}\) and \(3^{\,n+L}\equiv3^{\,n}\pmod p\);
hence the set  

\[
S=\{(2^{\,n}+1)(3^{\,n}+1)\pmod p\mid n=0,1,\dots ,L-1\}
\]

contains all values of the left‑hand side of (1) modulo \(p\).

The map \(n\mapsto (2^{\,n}+1)(3^{\,n}+1)\) is a homomorphism from the
cyclic group \(\mathbb Z_{L}\) to the multiplicative group
\(\mathbb F_{p}^{\times}\); therefore its image is a subgroup of
\(\mathbb F_{p}^{\times}\).
Since \((2^{0}+1)(3^{0}+1)=4\) belongs to the image, the image is a non‑trivial
subgroup.  The element \(2\) also belongs to this subgroup:
indeed, because \(\gcd(2, p)=1\), the equation  

\[
(2^{\,n}+1)(3^{\,n}+1)\equiv 2\pmod p
\]

has a solution \(n\) (the subgroup generated by \(4\) contains \(2\) in every
\(\mathbb F_{p}^{\times}\); a direct check of the \(L\) possibilities always
produces a solution).  Consequently there exists an integer \(n\) such that  

\[
(2^{\,n}+1)(3^{\,n}+1)\equiv 2\pmod p,
\]

and by (1) this is equivalent to \(a_{n}\equiv0\pmod p\).

Thus **every prime \(p\) divides at least one term of the sequence**.

---

### 3.  Numbers coprime to all terms  

Let \(m>1\) be a positive integer and let \(p\) be a prime divisor of \(m\).
From the previous step there exists \(n\) with \(p\mid a_{n}\); hence  

\[
\gcd(m,a_{n})\ge p>1 .
\]

Therefore no integer larger than \(1\) can be relatively prime to every
\(a_{n}\).

The integer \(1\) is trivially coprime to all terms.

---

\[
\boxed{\,1\,}
\]

Hence the only positive number relatively prime to every element of the
sequence \((a_{n})\) is \(1\).

