### 2.  Every prime divides some term  

Let \(p\) be a prime.

* **\(p=2\).**  \(a_{1}=10\) is even, so \(2\mid a_{1}\).

* **\(p=3\).**  \(a_{2}=48\) is a multiple of \(3\).

* **\(p\ge 5\).**  
  Reduce the closed form modulo \(p\):

  \[
  a_{n}\equiv 6^{\,n}+2^{\,n}+3^{\,n}-1\pmod p .
  \]

  Because \(p\neq2,3\), the numbers \(2,3,6\) are units in \(\mathbb F_{p}\) and
  each of the sequences \(2^{\,n},3^{\,n},6^{\,n}\) is periodic with period
  dividing \(p-1\).  Hence the map  

  \[
  f:\; \{0,1,\dots ,p-2\}\longrightarrow\mathbb F_{p},\qquad
  f(n)=6^{\,n}+2^{\,n}+3^{\,n}-1
  \]

  takes at most \(p-1\) different values.  
  Consider the sum of all its values:

  \[
  \sum_{n=0}^{p-2}f(n)=\sum_{n=0}^{p-2}6^{\,n}
                     +\sum_{n=0}^{p-2}2^{\,n}
                     +\sum_{n=0}^{p-2}3^{\,n}
                     -(p-1).
  \]

  For any \(r\not\equiv1\pmod p\) we have
  \(\displaystyle\sum_{n=0}^{p-2}r^{\,n}=0\) (geometric series and
  Fermat’s little theorem).  Since \(p\neq5\), none of \(2,3,6\) is
  congruent to \(1\) modulo \(p\); consequently the three sums are all
  \(0\).  Thus  

  \[
  \sum_{n=0}^{p-2}f(n)\equiv -(p-1)\equiv 1\pmod p .
  \]

  The sum of the \(p-1\) values is congruent to \(1\), which is impossible
  if none of the values were \(0\) (otherwise the sum would be a sum of
  non‑zero residues and could not be \(1\)).  Hence there exists
