### 2.  Every odd prime divides some term  

Let \(p\) be an odd prime.  
Write the three bases as powers of a primitive root \(g\) modulo \(p\):

\[
2\equiv g^{a},\qquad 3\equiv g^{b}\qquad (0<a,b<p-1).
\]

Because \(-1\equiv g^{\frac{p-1}{2}}\), we have  

\[
-2\equiv g^{a+\frac{p-1}{2}},\qquad -3\equiv g^{b+\frac{p-1}{2}} .
\tag{2}
\]

Consider the simultaneous congruences  

\[
\begin{cases}
2^{\,n}\equiv -3 \pmod p,\\[2mm]
3^{\,n}\equiv -2 \pmod p .
\end{cases}
\tag{3}
\]

Using (2) they become  

\[
\begin{cases}
a n\equiv b+\dfrac{p-1}{2}\pmod{p-1},\\[2mm]
b n\equiv a+\dfrac{p-1}{2}\pmod{p-1}.
\end{cases}
\tag{4}
\]

Since \(\gcd(a,p-1)=\gcd(b,p-1)=1\) (otherwise \(2\) or \(3\) would be
\(0\) modulo \(p\)), each congruence in (4) has a unique solution modulo
\(p-1\).  The two solutions are compatible because subtracting the
first from the second gives  

\[
(b-a)n\equiv (a-b)\pmod{p-1},
\]

which is always true.  Hence there exists an integer \(n\) satisfying
(3).

For such an \(n\),

\[
2^{\,n}+3^{\,n}+6^{\,n}
   \equiv -3-2+6\equiv 1\pmod p,
\]

and by (1)

\[
a_{n}=6^{\,n}+2^{\,n}+3^{\,n}-1\equiv 0\pmod p .
\]

Thus **every odd prime \(p\) divides at least one term of the sequence**.
Together with the observation that \(2\mid a_{n}\) for all \(n\) (because
\(6^{n},2^{n},3^{n}\) are all even), we have:

\[
\forall\; \text{prime }p\;(p\ge 2),\; p\mid a_{k}\ \text{for some }k .
\tag{5}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Numbers coprime to all terms  

Let \(m>1\) be a positive integer and let \(p\) be any prime divisor of
\(m\).  By (5) there exists an index \(k\) with \(p\mid a_{k}\); consequently  

\[
\gcd(m,a_{k})\ge p>1 .
\]

Hence \(m\) cannot be relatively prime to **every** term of the
sequence.  The only positive integer that has no prime divisor is \(1\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
\[
\boxed{\,\text{The only positive integer relatively prime to all }a_{n}
\text{ is }1.\,}
\]

