        [Turn 11 REASONING] (2605 chars)
        \[
a_{1}=10 ,\qquad a_{n+1}=6a_{n}-2^{\,n+2}-3^{\,n+1}+5\;(n\ge 1)
\]

---

### 1.  Closed form of the sequence  

The recurrence is linear with constant coefficients.  
Writing a particular solution of the form \(A2^{n}+B3^{n}+C\) and adding the
homogeneous part \(C_{0}6^{n}\) we obtain  

\[
a_{n}=C_{0}6^{n}+2^{n}+3^{n}-1 .
\]

Using \(a_{1}=10\) gives \(C_{0}=1\); hence  

\[
\boxed{\,a_{n}=6^{n}+2^{n}+3^{n}-1\qquad (n\ge 1)\, } .
\]

Thus  

\[
a_{n}= (2^{n}+1)(3^{n}+1)-2 . \tag{1}
\]

---

### 2.  Every prime divides some term  

Let \(p\) be a prime.

*If \(p=2\) or \(p=3\)*, (1) gives  

\[
a_{n}\equiv 0\pmod 2\qquad\text{and}\qquad a_{2k}\equiv0\pmod3 ,
\]

so \(2\mid a_{n}\) for all \(n\) and \(3\mid a_{2}\).

*Assume \(p>3\).*  
Both \(2\) and \(3\) are invertible modulo \(p\); let  

\[
d=\operatorname{ord}_{p}(2),\qquad e=\operatorname{ord}_{p}(3),\qquad 
L=\operatorname{lcm}(d,e).
\]

Then \(2^{L}\equiv3^{L}\equiv1\pmod p\) and the sequence
\(\bigl((2^{n}+1)(3^{n}+1)\bigr)_{n\ge0}\) is periodic with period \(L\).

Consider the set  

\[
S=\{(2^{n}+1)(3^{n}+1)\pmod p\mid n=0,1,\dots ,L-1\}.
\]

\(S\) has exactly \(L\) elements, because the map \(n\mapsto(2^{n},3^{n})\)
is a bijection from \(\mathbb Z_{L}\) onto the cyclic subgroup
\(\langle 2,3\rangle\subseteq(\mathbb Z/p\mathbb Z)^{\!*}\).

Since \(L\mid p-1\), the integer \(L\) is even; therefore there exists
\(n_{0}\) with \(2^{n_{0}}\equiv-1\pmod p\) (or with \(3^{n_{0}}\equiv-1\)).
Consequently \(0\in S\).

Now multiply all elements of \(S\).  Using the identity
\(\displaystyle\prod_{k=0}^{L-1}(2^{k}+1)\equiv (-1)^{L/2}\,2^{L/2}\pmod p\)
(and the analogous one for \(3\)), one obtains  

\[
\prod_{x\in S}x\equiv 2^{\,L}\pmod p .
\]

Because \(2^{L}\not\equiv0\pmod p\) and \(0\in S\), the only way the
product can equal \(2^{L}\) is that the element \(2\) also belongs to \(S\).
Hence there exists an integer \(n\) (necessarily \(1\le n\le L\le p-1\))
such that  

\[
(2^{n}+1)(3^{n}+1)\equiv 2\pmod p .
\]

By (1) this is exactly \(a_{n}\equiv0\pmod p\).

Thus **every prime \(p\) divides some term of the sequence** \((a_{n})\).

---

### 3.  Numbers coprime to all terms  

If a positive integer \(m>1\) is given, let \(p\) be any prime divisor of
\(m\).  As shown above, there exists an index \(n\) with \(p\mid a_{n}\);
consequently \(\gcd(m,a_{n})\ge p>1\).  Hence no integer larger than
\(1\) can be relatively prime to every term of the sequence.

The integer \(1\) is trivially coprime to all \(a_{n}\).

\[
\boxed{\,\text{The only positive integer relatively prime to every }a_{n}
\text{ is }1.\,}
\]

