        \[
a_{1}=10 ,\qquad a_{n+1}=6a_{n}-2^{\,n+2}-3^{\,n+1}+5\qquad (n\ge 1)
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  Closed form of the sequence  

Write the recurrence as  

\[
a_{n+1}-6a_{n}= -2^{\,n+2}-3^{\,n+1}+5 .
\]

A particular solution of the form \(A\cdot2^{n}+B\cdot3^{n}+C\) gives  

\[
-4A\cdot2^{n}-3B\cdot3^{n}-5C=-4\cdot2^{n}-3\cdot3^{n}+5 ,
\]

hence \(A=B=1,\;C=-1\).  
The homogeneous part is \(a_{n}^{(h)}=C\cdot6^{\,n-1}\).  
Using \(a_{1}=10\) we obtain \(C=6\).  

Thus for every \(n\ge 1\)

\[
\boxed{\,a_{n}=6^{\,n}+2^{\,n}+3^{\,n}-1\,}
      = (2^{\,n}+1)(3^{\,n}+1)-2 .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Every prime divides a term of the sequence  

*For \(p=2,3\)* :  

\(a_{n}=6^{n}+2^{n}+3^{n}-1\) is even for all \(n\), so \(2\mid a_{n}\).  
For even \(n\), \(a_{n}\equiv0\pmod 3\); hence \(3\mid a_{n}\).

*Let \(p>3\) be a prime.*  
Denote by \(\operatorname{ord}_{p}(2)\) and \(\operatorname{ord}_{p}(3)\) the
multiplicative orders of \(2\) and \(3\) modulo \(p\) and put  

\[
d=\operatorname{lcm}\bigl(\operatorname{ord}_{p}(2),\operatorname{ord}_{p}(3)\bigr).
\]

Then \(2^{d}\equiv3^{d}\equiv1\pmod p\); consequently the sequence
\((a_{n})_{n\ge0}\) is periodic modulo \(p\) with period dividing \(d\).

Consider the product over one full period:

\[
P=\prod_{k=0}^{d-1} a_{k}
   =\prod_{k=0}^{d-1}\bigl((2^{k}+1)(3^{k}+1)-2\bigr)\pmod p .
\]

Write \(A=\prod_{k=0}^{d-1}(2^{k}+1)\) and \(B=\prod_{k=0}^{d-1}(3^{k}+1)\).
Because \(2^{\operatorname{ord}_{p}(2)}\equiv1\), the set
\(\{2^{k}\mid 0\le k<d\}\) consists of each element of the subgroup
\(\langle2\rangle\) exactly \(d/\operatorname{ord}_{p}(2)\) times, and similarly
for \(3\).  Using the identity  

\[
\prod_{j=0}^{m-1}(1+g^{j}) = 1-(-1)^{m}\qquad
\bigl(g\text{ a generator of a cyclic group of order }m\bigr)
\]

we obtain  

\[
A =\bigl(1-(-1)^{\operatorname{ord}_{p}(2)}\bigr)^{\,d/\operatorname{ord}_{p}(2)},
\qquad
B =\bigl(1-(-1)^{\operatorname{ord}_{p}(3)}\bigr)^{\,d/\operatorname{ord}_{p}(3)} .
\]

Since \(p>3\) is odd, \(p-1\) is even; therefore at least one of the
orders \(\operatorname{ord}_{p}(2),\operatorname{ord}_{p}(3)\) is even.
If \(\operatorname{ord}_{p}(2)\) is even, then \(1-(-1)^{\operatorname{ord}_{p}(2)}=0\)
and consequently \(A\equiv0\pmod p\); the same holds when
\(\operatorname{ord}_{p}(3)\) is even.  Hence \(A\equiv0\) or \(B\equiv0\),
and therefore

\[
\prod_{k=0}^{d-1}\bigl((2^{k}+1)(3^{k}+1)\bigr)\equiv0\pmod p .
\]

Because each factor of the product is \((2^{k}+1)(3^{k}+1)=a_{k}+2\),
the product \(\prod_{k=0}^{d-1}(a_{k}+2)\) is \(0\) modulo \(p\).
If none of the \(a_{k}\) were \(0\) modulo \(p\), then every factor
\(a_{k}+2\) would be non‑zero, and their product could not be \(0\).
Thus some index \(k\) (with \(0\le k<d\)) satisfies \(a_{k}\equiv0\pmod p\).

Consequently **every prime \(p\) divides at least one term of the sequence**.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Numbers coprime to all terms

Let \(m>1\) be a positive integer and let \(q\) be a prime divisor of \(m\).
From the previous paragraph there exists an index \(n\) with
\(a_{n}\equiv0\pmod q\); hence \(\gcd(m,a_{n})\ge q>1\).
Therefore no integer larger than \(1\) can be relatively prime to every
\(a_{n}\).

The integer \(1\) is trivially coprime to all terms.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
\[
\boxed{\,\text{The only positive integer relatively prime to every }a_{n}
       \text{ is }1.\,}
\]

