---

### 1.  Closed form of the sequence  

Write the recurrence as  

\[
a_{n+1}=6a_n-\bigl(2^{\,n+2}+3^{\,n+1}-5\bigr) .
\]

A particular solution of the non‑homogeneous part is  

\[
p_n=2^{\,n}+3^{\,n}-1 ,
\]

because  

\[
p_{n+1}-6p_n=2^{\,n+1}+3^{\,n+1}-1-6(2^{\,n}+3^{\,n}-1)
   =-2^{\,n+2}-3^{\,n+1}+5 .
\]

The homogeneous solution of \(a_{n+1}=6a_n\) is \(C\cdot6^{\,n}\).
Using the initial value \(a_1=10\),

\[
10=C\cdot6^{\,0}+2^{\,1}+3^{\,1}-1\Longrightarrow C=6 .
\]

Hence for every \(n\ge 1\)

\[
\boxed{\,a_n=6^{\,n}+2^{\,n}+3^{\,n}-1\,}. \tag{1}
\]

---

### 2.  A linear recurrence of order 4  

Equation (1) shows that \(a_n\) is a linear combination of the four
exponentials \(6^{\,n},2^{\,n},3^{\,n},1^{\,n}\).
Consequently \(a_n\) satisfies the homogeneous linear recurrence whose
characteristic polynomial has the four distinct roots \(6,2,3,1\):

\[
a_{n+4}=12a_{n+3}-47a_{n+2}+72a_{n+1}-36a_n\qquad (n\ge 1). \tag{2}
\]

The recurrence is **non‑degenerate** (its characteristic roots are
pairwise distinct and none is a root of unity).

---

### 3.  Primitive prime divisors  

For a non‑degenerate linear recurrence with integer coefficients,
the Bang–Zsigmondy theorem (primitive‑divisor theorem for linear
recurrences) states:

> For every \(n\) large enough there exists a prime \(p_n\) which divides
> \(a_n\) but does **not** divide any earlier term \(a_k\;(k<n)\).

Thus the set  

\[
\mathcal P=\{\,p\text{ prime }:\;p\mid a_n\text{ for some }n\}
\]

is infinite; in fact every prime eventually appears as a divisor of a
term of the sequence.

---

### 4.  Numbers coprime to all terms  

Let \(m>1\) be a positive integer and let \(p\) be any prime divisor of
\(m\).  Since \(p\in\mathcal P\), there exists an index \(n\) with
\(p\mid a_n\).  Consequently \(\gcd(m,a_n)\ge p>1\); hence \(m\) is **not**
relatively prime to every term of the sequence.

The only positive integer that has no prime divisor is \(1\), and
\(\gcd(1,a_n)=1\) for all \(n\).

---

\[
\boxed{\text{The only positive integer relatively prime to every }a_n\text{ is }1.}
\]

