--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  Closed form of the sequence  

The recurrence is first‑order linear:

\[
a_{n+1}=6a_{n}+f(n),\qquad 
f(n)=-2^{\,n+2}-3^{\,n+1}+5 .
\]

Solving it gives  

\[
\begin{aligned}
a_{n}
&=6^{\,n-1}a_{1}+\sum_{k=1}^{n-1}6^{\,n-1-k}f(k)  \\
&=10\cdot6^{\,n-1}
      -\sum_{k=1}^{n-1}6^{\,n-1-k}2^{\,k+2}
      -\sum_{k=1}^{n-1}6^{\,n-1-k}3^{\,k+1}
      +5\sum_{k=1}^{n-1}6^{\,n-1-k}.
\end{aligned}
\]

Evaluating the three geometric sums gives  

\[
a_{n}=6^{\,n}+2^{\,n}+3^{\,n}-1\qquad (n\ge 1).
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  What numbers can be coprime to every term?

Let \(m>1\) be an integer and let \(p\) be a prime divisor of \(m\).
If we can show that for **every** prime \(p\) there exists an index
\(n\) with  

\[
p\mid a_{n}=6^{\,n}+2^{\,n}+3^{\,n}-1,
\]

then \(p\) would divide a term of the sequence, whence
\(\gcd(m,a_{n})\neq1\).  
Consequently the only positive integer that can be relatively prime to
all the \(a_{n}\) is \(1\).

Thus we have to prove:

> **Lemma.** For every prime \(p\) there exists \(n\ge1\) such that  
> \(6^{\,n}+2^{\,n}+3^{\,n}\equiv1\pmod p\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Proof of the lemma

*The primes \(p=2,3\).*  
\(a_{n}=6^{\,n}+2^{\,n}+3^{\,n}-1\) is even for all \(n\); hence
\(2\mid a_{n}\).  
Modulo \(3\) we have \(a_{n}\equiv2^{\,n}-1\); for even \(n\),
\(2^{\,n}\equiv1\pmod3\) and therefore \(3\mid a_{n}\).

*Odd primes \(p\ge5\).*  
Let \(g\) be a primitive root modulo \(p\).  
There exist integers \(\alpha,\beta\;(1\le\alpha,\beta\le p-2)\) such that  

\[
2\equiv g^{\alpha},\qquad 3\equiv g^{\beta}\pmod p .
\]

Write \(t=g^{n}\;(n=0,1,\dots ,p-2)\); then \(t\) runs through all
non‑zero residues modulo \(p\).  Using the closed form of \(a_{n}\),

\[
a_{n}\equiv g^{\alpha n}+g^{\beta n}+g^{(\alpha+\beta)n}-1
      =(t^{\alpha}+1)(t^{\beta}+1)-2\pmod p .
\]

Define the polynomial  

\[
F(t)=(t^{\alpha}+1)(t^{\beta}+1)-2
      =t^{\alpha+\beta}+t^{\alpha}+t^{\beta}-1\in\mathbb{F}_{p}[t].
\]

Its degree is \(\alpha+\beta\le 2(p-2)<p-1\); therefore
\(F\) is **not** a permutation polynomial of \(\mathbb{F}_{p}\)
(a permutation polynomial of degree \(<p-1\) must be of the form
\(c\,t^{k}+d\) with \(c\neq0\)).  
Consequently \(F\) has a root in \(\mathbb{F}_{p}^{\*}\); i.e. there exists
\(t_{0}\neq0\) with \(F(t_{0})\equiv0\pmod p\).

Because \(g\) is a primitive root, there is an integer \(n\) such that
\(t_{0}=g^{n}\).  For this \(n\),

\[
a_{n}\equiv F(g^{n})\equiv0\pmod p .
\]

Thus for every odd prime \(p\) a term of the sequence is divisible by
\(p\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  Conclusion

Every prime divisor of any integer \(m>1\) divides some term of the
sequence \((a_{n})\).  Hence no integer larger than \(1\) can be
relatively prime to **all** the numbers \(a_{n}\).

\[
\boxed{\,1\,}
\]

The only positive integer that is relatively prime to every term of the
sequence is \(1\).

