        [Turn 7 REASONING] (3332 chars)
        \[
a_{1}=10,\qquad a_{n+1}=6a_{n}-2^{\,n+2}-3^{\,n+1}+5\;(n\ge 1)
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  Closed form of the sequence  

Write the recurrence as  

\[
a_{n+1}=6a_{n}-\bigl(2^{\,n+2}+3^{\,n+1}-5\bigr).
\]

The homogeneous part has solution \(C\cdot6^{\,n}\).
A particular solution of the form \(A\cdot2^{\,n}+B\cdot3^{\,n}+C\) gives  

\[
\begin{aligned}
A\cdot2^{\,n+1}+B\cdot3^{\,n+1}+C
   &=6\bigl(A\cdot2^{\,n}+B\cdot3^{\,n}+C\bigr)
     -2^{\,n+2}-3^{\,n+1}+5 .
\end{aligned}
\]

Comparing coefficients yields \(A=1,\;B=1,\;C=-1\).  
Hence

\[
a_{n}=6^{\,n}+2^{\,n}+3^{\,n}-1\qquad (n\ge 1).      \tag{1}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  A prime divisor of a term  

Let \(p\) be a prime.  
For \(p=2\) we have \(a_{n}\equiv0\pmod 2\) for every \(n\).  
For \(p=3\) we have \(a_{n}\equiv0\pmod 3\) whenever \(n\) is even.
Thus both \(2\) and \(3\) certainly divide some term of the sequence.

Assume now that \(p>3\).  
Because \(p\) does not divide \(2,3,6\), the numbers
\(2^{\,n},3^{\,n},6^{\,n}\) are periodic modulo \(p\) with period
\(\ell=\operatorname{lcm}\bigl(\operatorname{ord}_p2,\operatorname{ord}_p3\bigr)\),
where \(\operatorname{ord}_p r\) denotes the multiplicative order of \(r\) modulo \(p\).

Define  

\[
f(n)=\bigl(2^{\,n}+1\bigr)\bigl(3^{\,n}+1\bigl)\pmod p .
\]

From (1),

\[
a_{n}\equiv0\pmod p\iff f(n)\equiv2\pmod p . \tag{2}
\]

The map \(n\mapsto f(n)\) is a map from the cyclic group
\(\mathbb Z_{\ell}\) into the multiplicative group \(\mathbb F_{p}^{\times}\).
Consequently the set  

\[
S=\{f(n)\mid n=0,1,\dots ,\ell-1\}
\]

is a subgroup of \(\mathbb F_{p}^{\times}\) (the product of two elements of
\(S\) is again an element of \(S\) because
\(f(i+j)=\bigl(2^{\,i}2^{\,j}+1\bigr)\bigl(3^{\,i}3^{\,j}+1\bigr)
        =f(i)f(j)\) in \(\mathbb F_{p}^{\times}\)).
Hence \(S\) is a non‑trivial subgroup of \(\mathbb F_{p}^{\times}\).

Now \(f(0)=(2^{0}+1)(3^{0}+1)=4\in S\); therefore \(4\in S\).
If \(4\) were the whole subgroup, then every element of \(S\) would be a
power of \(4\).  Because \(\ell\ge2\), there exists an integer
\(k\) with \(0<k<\ell\) such that \(f(k)\neq4\); in particular
\(f(k)\) is a non‑trivial element of the subgroup generated by \(4\).
Consequently the subgroup generated by the two distinct elements
\(4\) and \(f(k)\) is the whole multiplicative group
\(\mathbb F_{p}^{\times}\).  Hence \(2\in S\).

Thus there exists an integer \(n\) (necessarily \(0\le n<\ell\)) with
\(f(n)\equiv2\pmod p\); by (2) this \(n\) satisfies
\(a_{n}\equiv0\pmod p\).

We have proved:

> **For every prime \(p\) there is a positive integer \(n\) such that
> \(p\mid a_{n}\).**

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Numbers coprime to all terms  

Let \(m>1\) be a positive integer and let \(p\) be any prime divisor of
\(m\).  From the previous paragraph there exists \(n\) with
\(p\mid a_{n}\); consequently \(\gcd(m,a_{n})\ge p>1\).
Hence no integer larger than \(1\) can be relatively prime to every term
of the sequence.

The integer \(1\) is trivially relatively prime to every integer, and
there is no other positive integer with this property.

