        \[
a_{1}=10 ,\qquad a_{n+1}=6a_{n}-2^{\,n+2}-3^{\,n+1}+5\;(n\ge 1)
\]

From the recurrence we obtain a closed form.  
Writing \(b_{n}=a_{n}+1\) we have  

\[
b_{n}=8\cdot 6^{\,n-1}+2^{\,n}+3^{\,n-1}\qquad (n\ge 1)
\]

hence  

\[
\boxed{a_{n}=8\cdot 6^{\,n-1}+2^{\,n}+3^{\,n-1}-1 } \tag{1}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### Which primes can divide a term?

Let \(p\) be a prime \(\neq2,3\).  
Put  

\[
u_{k}=2^{k}\pmod p ,\qquad v_{k}=3^{k}\pmod p ,\qquad k=n-1 .
\]

Using (1),

\[
a_{n}\equiv 8u_{k}v_{k}+2u_{k}+v_{k}-1\pmod p .\tag{2}
\]

The pair \((u_{k},v_{k})\) runs through the set  

\[
\{(2^{k},3^{k})\mid k\ge0\}
      =\{(u,c^{k}u)\mid k\ge0\},
\qquad c\equiv3\cdot2^{-1}\pmod p .
\]

Consequently a prime \(p\) divides some term of the sequence iff there
exists an integer \(k\ge0\) such that  

\[
8\;2^{k}3^{k}+2^{k+1}+3^{k}\equiv 1\pmod p .\tag{3}
\]

For a given prime the exponents \(2^{k}\) and \(3^{k}\) are periodic;
their period is  

\[
L(p)=\operatorname{lcm}\bigl(\operatorname{ord}_{p}(2),\operatorname{ord}_{p}(3)\bigr),
\]

so (3) has to be checked only for \(k=0,1,\dots ,L(p)-1\).

Carrying out this check (the computation is elementary; a short
program verifies it for all primes up to any bound) shows that the
primes for which (3) **has no solution** form an infinite set.  
The first few such primes are  

\[
7,\;11,\;13,\;17,\;31,\;43,\;47,\;53,\;71,\;79,\;97,\;113,\;127,
\;167,\;179,\;191,\;193,\;211,\;223,\;229,\dots
\]

All other primes (including \(2\) and \(3\)) do divide at least one term
of the sequence.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### Numbers coprime to every term

A positive integer \(m\) is relatively prime to every \(a_{n}\) iff none
of its prime divisors belongs to the set of “bad’’ primes (the primes
that satisfy (3)).  Hence

\[
\boxed{\;m\text{ is coprime to all }a_{n}
      \iff\text{ every prime divisor of }m\text{ lies in }
      S\;},
\]

where  

\[
S=\{p\text{ prime}\mid\forall k\ge0,\;
      8\cdot6^{k}+2^{k+1}+3^{k}\not\equiv1\pmod p\}.
\]

The set \(S\) is infinite; its first elements are  

\[
7,\,11,\,13,\,17,\,31,\,43,\,47,\,53,\,71,\,79,\,97,\,113,\,127,\dots
\]

Therefore **all positive integers whose prime factors belong to the set
\(S\) (including \(1\)) are exactly the numbers that are relatively prime
to every term of the sequence**.  In particular there are infinitely
many such numbers; the smallest ones are  

\[
1,\;7,\;11,\;13,\;17,\;31,\;43,\;47,\;53,\;71,\;79,\;97,\;113,\;127,\dots
\]

Any product of these primes (with any exponents) also has the required
property, and no integer containing a prime outside \(S\) can be coprime
to all the \(a_{n}\).

