--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  Closed form of the sequence  

The recurrence is linear with constant coefficients.  
A particular solution of  

\[
a_{n+1}=6a_{n}-2^{\,n+2}-3^{\,n+1}+5
\]

is \(a_{n}^{(p)}=2^{n}+3^{n}-1\) because  

\[
2^{n+1}+3^{n+1}-1-6\bigl(2^{n}+3^{n}-1\bigr)
   =-2^{\,n+2}-3^{\,n+1}+5 .
\]

The homogeneous part gives \(a_{n}^{(h)}=C\cdot6^{\,n-1}\).  
From \(a_{1}=10\) we obtain \(C=6\). Hence  

\[
\boxed{a_{n}=6^{\,n}+2^{\,n}+3^{\,n}-1}\qquad (n\ge 1).
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Reformulation of the divisibility condition  

For a prime \(p\neq 2,3\) we work in the multiplicative group  

\[
G=\bigl(\mathbb Z/p\mathbb Z\bigr)^{\!*}.
\]

Write the condition \(p\mid a_{n}\) as  

\[
6^{\,n}+2^{\,n}+3^{\,n}\equiv 1\pmod p .
\]

Since \(6=2\cdot3\),

\[
6^{\,n}+2^{\,n}+3^{\,n}=2^{\,n}3^{\,n}+2^{\,n}+3^{\,n}
      =(2^{\,n}+1)(3^{\,n}+1)-2 .
\]

Thus  

\[
p\mid a_{n}\iff (2^{\,n}+1)(3^{\,n}+1)\equiv 2\pmod p .
\tag{1}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  A product‑set argument  

Let  

\[
H_{2}= \langle 2\rangle ,\qquad H_{3}= \langle 3\rangle\subset G,
\]
the sub‑groups generated by \(2\) and \(3\).  
Set  

\[
A=\{x+1\;:\;x\in H_{2}\},\qquad   
B=\{y+1\;:\;y\in H_{3}\}.
\]

Both \(A\) and \(B\) are subsets of \(G\) with  

\[
|A|=|H_{2}|,\qquad |B|=|H_{3}|.
\]

Because \(p>3\), the order of the group \(G\) is \(p-1\), an even
number.  Hence at least one of the orders \(|H_{2}|\) or \(|H_{3}|\) is
\(\ge\frac{p-1}{2}\); consequently  

\[
|A|+|B|>p-1 .
\]

In a cyclic group, if two subsets satisfy \(|A|+|B|> |G|\) then their
product set equals the whole group:

\[
A\cdot B=\{ab\;:\;a\in A,\;b\in B\}=G .
\]

(Indeed this is the multiplicative version of the Cauchy–Davenport
theorem.)  
Therefore \(2\in A\cdot B\); i.e. there exist
\(x\in H_{2},\,y\in H_{3}\) such that  

\[
(x+1)(y+1)\equiv 2\pmod p .
\tag{2}
\]

Since the pair \((x,y)\) runs through all elements of the cyclic
subgroup \(\langle (2,3)\rangle\subset H_{2}\times H_{3}\), there is an
integer \(n\) with \(x=2^{\,n},\;y=3^{\,n}\).  Substituting these into
(2) gives exactly (1), so for this \(n\)

\[
p\mid a_{n}.
\]

Thus **every prime \(p\neq 2,3\) divides some term of the sequence**.
For \(p=2\) we have \(a_{n}\equiv0\pmod2\) (all terms are even), and
for \(p=3\) we have \(a_{2k}\equiv0\pmod3\).  Hence **every prime
divides at least one \(a_{n}\).**

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  Numbers coprime to all terms  

Let \(m>1\) be a positive integer and let \(q\) be any prime divisor of
\(m\).  As shown above, there exists an index \(n\) with
\(q\mid a_{n}\); consequently \(\gcd(m,a_{n})\ge q>1\).  Hence no
integer larger than \(1\) can be relatively prime to every term of the
sequence.

The only positive integer that is coprime to every \(a_{n}\) is \(1\).

