--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  Closed form of the sequence  

The recurrence is linear with constant coefficient \(6\).  
Writing it as  

\[
a_{n+1}=6a_{n}+(-2^{\,n+2}-3^{\,n+1}+5)
\]

and solving it gives  

\[
\begin{aligned}
a_{n}&=6^{\,n-1}a_{1}
        +\sum_{k=1}^{n-1}6^{\,n-1-k}\bigl(-2^{\,k+2}-3^{\,k+1}+5\bigr)\\[2mm]
     &=6^{\,n}+2^{\,n}+3^{\,n}-1\qquad (n\ge 1).
\end{aligned}
\]

Thus  

\[
\boxed{\,a_{n}=6^{n}+2^{n}+3^{n}-1\, } .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Every prime divides a term of the sequence  

Let \(p\) be a prime.  
If \(p=2\) or \(p=3\) we have immediately  

\[
a_{n}\equiv0\pmod{2},\qquad a_{2k}\equiv0\pmod{3},
\]

so \(p\mid a_{n}\) for some \(n\).

Assume now \(p>3\).  
The multiplicative group \(\mathbb F_{p}^{\*}\) is cyclic; let \(g\) be a primitive
root modulo \(p\).  Write  

\[
2\equiv g^{r},\qquad 3\equiv g^{s}\qquad (1\le r,s\le p-2).
\]

Then  

\[
a_{n}\equiv g^{(r+s)n}+g^{rn}+g^{sn}-1\pmod{p}.
\]

Define the polynomial  

\[
F(X)=X^{\,r+s}+X^{\,r}+X^{\,s}-1\in\mathbb F_{p}[X].
\]

Its degree is at most \(p-2\) (because \(r,s,r+s\not\equiv0\pmod{p-1}\); the only
exception is \(p=5\), which is handled directly: \(a_{1}=10\equiv0\pmod5\)).  

Consider the product of the values of \(F\) over the whole multiplicative
group:

\[
P=\prod_{x\in\mathbb F_{p}^{\*}}F(x).
\]

Since \(F\) is a non‑zero polynomial of degree \(<p-1\), the product \(P\) equals
the resultant of \(F\) and \(X^{p-1}-1\).  Because \(X^{p-1}-1\) has the
\(p-1\) distinct roots \(x\in\mathbb F_{p}^{\*}\), the resultant is
\(0\) precisely when \(F\) has a common root with \(X^{p-1}-1\); i.e. when
\(F(x)=0\) for some \(x\in\mathbb F_{p}^{\*}\).  But

\[
P\equiv\prod_{x\in\mathbb F_{p}^{\*}}(x^{\,r+s}+x^{\,r}+x^{\,s}-1)
      =\prod_{x\in\mathbb F_{p}^{\*}}(x^{\,r}+1)(x^{\,s}+1)-2^{\,p-1}
      \equiv0\pmod p,
\]

because the factor \((x^{\,r}+1)(x^{\,s}+1)-2\) is zero for \(x=1\) and the
remaining factors come in pairs \((x,\,x^{-1})\) whose product is \(1\).
Hence \(P\equiv0\pmod p\), so \(F\) has a root in \(\mathbb F_{p}^{\*}\).

Thus there exists \(t\in\mathbb F_{p}^{\*}\) with \(F(t)=0\).  Write
\(t=g^{n}\); then \(F(g^{n})=0\) gives

\[
g^{(r+s)n}+g^{rn}+g^{sn}\equiv1\pmod p,
\]

i.e. \(6^{n}+2^{n}+3^{n}\equiv1\pmod p\).  Consequently

\[
a_{n}=6^{n}+2^{n}+3^{n}-1\equiv0\pmod p .
\]

So for every prime \(p\) there exists a positive integer \(n\) with
\(p\mid a_{n}\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Numbers coprime to all terms  

Let \(m>1\) be a positive integer and let \(p\) be a prime divisor of \(m\).
From the previous paragraph there is an index \(n\) such that \(p\mid a_{n}\);
hence \(\gcd(m,a_{n})\ge p>1\).  Therefore \(m\) cannot be relatively
prime to every term of the sequence.

The only positive integer that has no prime divisor is \(1\).  Clearly
\(\gcd(1,a_{n})=1\) for all \(n\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
\[
\boxed{\,1\,}
\]

Thus the only positive number relatively prime to every element of the
sequence \((a_{n})\) is \(1\).

