--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  Every odd prime divides some term of the sequence  

Let \(p\) be an odd prime.  
Denote  

\[
d=\operatorname{ord}_{p}(2),\qquad e=\operatorname{ord}_{p}(3),
\]

the (multiplicative) orders of \(2\) and \(3\) modulo \(p\).
Both \(d\) and \(e\) are even, because they divide \(p-1\) (which is even) and
\(2\not\equiv1\pmod p,\;3\not\equiv1\pmod p\).

Put  

\[
L=\operatorname{lcm}(d,e).
\]

Then for every \(n\) the residues \(2^{n}\) and \(3^{n}\) run through the
sub‑groups  

\[
H=\{2^{0},2^{1},\dots ,2^{d-1}\},\qquad
K=\{3^{0},3^{1},\dots ,3^{e-1}\}
\]

each exactly \(L/d\) (resp. \(L/e\)) times when \(n\) runs from \(0\) to
\(L-1\).

Write  

\[
a_{n}+2=(2^{n}+1)(3^{n}+1).
\]

Hence

\[
\begin{aligned}
\prod_{n=0}^{L-1}(a_{n}+2)
   &=\Bigl(\prod_{h\in H}(h+1)^{L/d}\Bigr)
     \Bigl(\prod_{k\in K}(k+1)^{L/e}\Bigr) .
\end{aligned}
\]

Because \(d\) and \(e\) are even, \(-1\in H\) and \(-1\in K\); consequently
one factor in each product is \(0\) modulo \(p\).  Thus  

\[
\prod_{n=0}^{L-1}(a_{n}+2)\equiv0\pmod p .
\tag{1}
\]

Now expand the left–hand side of (1) as  

\[
\prod_{n=0}^{L-1}\bigl((a_{n}+2)-2\bigr)
   =\prod_{n=0}^{L-1}a_{n}\pmod p .
\]

If none of the \(a_{n}\) were divisible by \(p\), the product
\(\prod_{n=0}^{L-1}a_{n}\) would be non‑zero modulo \(p\); this would
contradict (1).  Hence for the chosen prime \(p\) there exists an index
\(n\) (with \(0\le n<L\)) such that  

\[
a_{n}\equiv0\pmod p .
\]

Thus **every odd prime divides at least one term of the sequence**.

