### 1.  Closed form of the sequence  

The recurrence is linear.  Solving it we obtain  

\[
\begin{aligned}
a_{n+1}&=6a_{n}-2^{\,n+2}-3^{\,n+1}+5\\
\Longrightarrow\;a_{n}&=6^{\,n}+2^{\,n}+3^{\,n}-1\qquad (n\ge 1).
\end{aligned}
\]

Indeed  

\[
\begin{aligned}
6^{\,n}+2^{\,n}+3^{\,n}-1
&=6\bigl(6^{\,n-1}+2^{\,n-1}+3^{\,n-1}-1\bigr)
      -2^{\,n+2}-3^{\,n+1}+5,
\end{aligned}
\]

and the initial value \(a_{1}=6+2+3-1=10\) is satisfied.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  What numbers can be coprime to every term?

Let \(m>1\) be an integer and let \(p\) be a prime divisor of \(m\).
If we can show that for **every** prime \(p\) there exists an index
\(n\) with  

\[
p\mid a_{n}=6^{\,n}+2^{\,n}+3^{\,n}-1,
\tag{1}
\]

then \(p\) divides a term of the sequence, and consequently \(m\) cannot be
coprime to all the \(a_{n}\).  
Hence the required numbers are exactly those whose prime factors never
divide any \(a_{n}\); if (1) holds for every prime, the only such integer
is \(1\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Every prime divides some term  

*The small primes.*  

\[
a_{1}=10\equiv0\pmod 2,\qquad a_{2}=48\equiv0\pmod 3,\qquad 
a_{1}=10\equiv0\pmod 5 .
\]

Thus \(p=2,3,5\) satisfy (1).

