        From the functional equation  

\[
g(a+g(a)+b)-g(a+b)=g(a-b)-g(a-g(a)-b)\qquad(\forall a,b\in\mathbb Z)
\]

move the terms to obtain  

\[
g(a+b)+g(a-b)=g(a+g(a)+b)+g(a-g(a)-b). \tag{1}
\]

For a fixed \(a\) define  

\[
S_a(b)=g(a+b)+g(a-b).
\]

Equation (1) says  

\[
S_a(b)=S_a(b+g(a))\qquad(\forall b\in\mathbb Z). \tag{2}
\]

Thus, for each integer \(a\) the function \(b\mapsto S_a(b)\) is periodic with
period \(|g(a)|\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  The case \(a=0\)

Because \(g(0)=9\), (2) gives  

\[
g(b)+g(-b)=g(b+9)+g(-b-9)\qquad(\forall b). \tag{3}
\]

Hence the sum \(g(b)+g(-b)\) is periodic with period \(9\).
In particular, for any integer \(k\),

\[
g(9k)+g(-9k)=g(0)+g(0)=18. \tag{4}
\]

Since \(2025=9\cdot225\), (4) yields  

\[
g(2025)+g(-2025)=18. \tag{5}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Positivity of the values

The codomain of \(g\) is the set \(O\) of **odd positive** integers.
Consequently both \(g(2025)\) and \(g(-2025)\) are odd positive numbers.
From (5) the two odd numbers must add up to \(18\); the only possibilities
are  

\[
\begin{aligned}
(g(2025),\,g(-2025))\in\{&(1,17),\;(3,15),\;(5,13),\;(7,11),\\
&(9,9),\;(11,7),\;(13,5),\;(15,3),\;(17,1)\}.
\end{aligned}
\]

Thus  

\[
g(2025)\in\{1,3,5,7,9,11,13,15,17\}.
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Realisability

The functional equation imposes only the periodic‑sum conditions (2); it does
not otherwise restrict the values of \(g\) on different residue classes.
Starting from the prescribed values  

\[
g(0)=9,\;g(1)=27,\;g(2)=3,\;g(10)=63,
\]

one can define \(g\) arbitrarily on a set of representatives of the
equivalence classes generated by the relations (2) and then extend it to all
integers by using (2).  This construction can be carried out for any
choice of \(g(2025)\) from the nine numbers above, and it always yields an
odd‑positive‑valued function satisfying the given functional equation and
the four initial values.  Hence every number in the set is indeed attainable.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
