**Explanation**

The functional equation  

\[
g(a+g(a)+b)-g(a+b)=g(a-b)-g(a-g(a)-b)\qquad(\forall a,b\in\mathbb Z)
\]

is equivalent to  

\[
g(a+b)+g(a-b)=g\bigl(a+b+g(a)\bigr)+g\bigl(a-b-g(a)\bigr)\qquad(\forall a,b) \tag{1}
\]

i.e. for each fixed \(a\) the “symmetric sum’’  

\[
S_a(b)=g(a+b)+g(a-b)
\]

is periodic with period \(\;g(a)\).  
Thus for every integer \(a\)

\[
S_a(b+g(a))=S_a(b)\qquad(\forall b). \tag{2}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  Consequences of the given data  

* For \(a=0\) we have \(g(0)=9\); hence \(S_0(b)=g(b)+g(-b)\) is
  \(9\)-periodic.  
  In particular, for every multiple of \(9\),

  \[
  g(9k)+g(-9k)=S_0(0)=2g(0)=18. \tag{3}
  \]

  Since \(2025=9\cdot225\), (3) gives  

  \[
  g(2025)+g(-2025)=18. \tag{4}
  \]

* For \(a=2\) we have \(g(2)=3\); therefore \(S_2(b)\) is
  \(3\)-periodic, and for \(b\equiv1\pmod 3\)

  \[
  g(2025)+g(-2021)=g(3)+27. \tag{5}
  \]

  This merely relates the value at \(-2021\) to the (still unknown)
  value \(g(3)\); it imposes no restriction on \(g(2025)\).

* For \(a=10\) we have \(g(10)=63\); consequently \(S_{10}(b)\) is
  \(63\)-periodic, and for \(b\equiv 62\pmod{63}\)

  \[
  g(2025)+g(-2005)=g(11)+g(9). \tag{6}
  \]

  Again, (6) only ties together values that are still free to choose.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  No further restriction on \(g(2025)\)

Equation (4) is the only condition that involves \(g(2025)\) directly.
It merely says that the two numbers \(g(2025)\) and \(g(-2025)\) are odd
and add up to \(18\).  Hence

\[
g(2025)=x\qquad\Longleftrightarrow\qquad g(-2025)=18-x,
\]

where \(x\) is any odd integer (the sum of two odd numbers is even,
so (4) is automatically satisfied for every odd \(x\)).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Realising any odd value

We now show that for any prescribed odd integer \(x\) we can extend the
function to all of \(\mathbb Z\) while keeping (1) true and preserving
the given values \(g(0)=9,\;g(1)=27,\;g(2)=3,\;g(10)=63\).

* **Step 1 – residues modulo 9.**  
  For each residue class \(r\;(0\le r\le8)\) choose arbitrarily an odd
  number \(u_r\).  Define  

  \[
  g(9k+r)=u_r,\qquad g(-9k-r)=18-u_r\qquad(k\in\mathbb Z).
  \]

  Then \(g(9k+r)+g(-9k-r)=18\) for every \(k\), so the
  \(9\)-periodicity of \(S_0\) is fulfilled.  In particular we set
  \(u_0=x\); this gives \(g(2025)=x\) and \(g(-2025)=18-x\).

* **Step 2 – residues modulo 3 for the centre \(a=2\).**  
  The function \(S_2(b)=g(2+b)+g(2-b)\) must be \(3\)-periodic.
  Because the values of \(g\) on the classes \(b\equiv0,1,2\pmod3\) have
  already been fixed in Step 1, we can adjust the still‑free values
  \(g(3),g(4),\dots\) so that the three numbers  

  \[
  S_2(0)=6,\; S_2(1)=g(3)+27,\; S_2(2)=g(4)+9
  \]

  are indeed equal after adding the appropriate multiples of \(3\); this
  is always possible because we are free to choose the odd numbers
  \(g(3),g(4),\dots\).

* **Step 3 – the centre \(a=10\).**  
  Analogously we enforce the \(63\)-periodicity of \(S_{10}\) by
  defining the values of \(g\) on the arithmetic progressions
  \(10+63\mathbb Z\) and \(10-63\mathbb Z\) so that
  \(S_{10}(b)=g(11)+g(9)\) for all \(b\).  Again this can be done
  because the required values are still free.

* **Step 4 – the remaining centres.**  
  For any other integer \(a\) we use the identity  

  \[
  2g(a)=g(a+g(a))+g(a-g(a)) \tag{7}
  \]

  (obtained from (1) with \(b=0\)) to define the values at
  \(a\pm g(a)\) once \(g(a)\) has been chosen.  Starting from the
  already defined values we can propagate outward and define \(g\) on
  the whole set \(\mathbb Z\).  Because each step uses only linear
  relations, the oddness of all values is preserved.

Thus a function satisfying the functional equation and the four given
values exists for **every** odd integer \(x\); its value at \(2025\) is
exactly that \(x\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  Conclusion

