**Explanation**

Write the functional equation as  

\[
g(a+g(a)+b)+g(a-g(a)-b)=g(a+b)+g(a-b)\qquad(\ast)
\]

For a fixed \(a\) define  

\[
H_a(x)=g(a+x)+g(a-x)\; .
\]

Then \((\ast)\) gives  

\[
H_a(x+g(a))=H_a(x)\qquad\text{for every }x\in\mathbb Z .
\tag{1}
\]

Thus for each integer \(a\) the sum \(g(a+x)+g(a-x)\) is periodic with period
\(g(a)\).

---

### 1.  Consequences of (1)

* For \(a=0\) we obtain a period \(g(0)=9\):
  \[
  g(n)+g(-n)=g(n+9)+g(-n-9)\qquad\forall n\in\mathbb Z .\tag{2}
  \]

  Hence the value of \(g(n)+g(-n)\) depends only on \(n\bmod 9\).
  In particular, because \(g(0)=9\),

  \[
  g(0)+g(0)=18\Longrightarrow g(9k)+g(-9k)=18\qquad\forall k\in\mathbb Z .
  \tag{3}
  \]

* For \(a=2\) we have \(g(2)=3\) and therefore a period \(3\):
  \[
  g(2+x)+g(2-x)=\begin{cases}
  6,&x\equiv0\pmod 3,\\[2mm]
  g(3)+27,&x\equiv1\pmod 3,\\[2mm]
  g(4)+9,&x\equiv2\pmod 3 .
  \end{cases}
  \tag{4}
  \]

  Taking \(x=2025\;( \equiv0\pmod3)\) gives
  \[
  g(2027)+g(-2023)=6. \tag{5}
  \]

* For \(a=1\) we have a period \(g(1)=27\); with \(x=1\) we obtain
  \[
  g(2)+g(0)=12 .\tag{6}
  \]

  With \(x=-1\;( \equiv26\pmod{27})\) we get
  \[
  g(2025)+g(-2023)=12 .\tag{7}
  \]

* For \(a=10\) we have a period \(g(10)=63\); with \(x=2016\;( \equiv0\pmod{63})\) we obtain
  \[
  g(2027)+g(-2025)=12 .\tag{8}
  \]

Together with (3) we have the four linear relations

\[
\begin{aligned}
g(2025)+g(-2025)&=18,\\
g(2025)+g(-2023)&=12,\\
g(-2023)+g(2027)&=6,\\
g(2027)+g(-2025)&=12 .
\end{aligned}
\tag{9}
\]

Solving (9) gives

\[
\begin{aligned}
g(2025)&=g(2027)+6,\\
g(-2025)&=12-g(2027),\\
g(-2023)&=6-g(2027).
\end{aligned}
\tag{10}
\]

Thus the value of \(g(2025)\) is completely determined by the single
integer \(g(2027)\).

---

### 2.  No further restriction on \(g(2027)\)

All the information we have about the function comes from the periodicity
relations (1).  Those relations involve only the *parities* of the
periods \(g(a)\); they impose no restriction on the actual magnitude of
\(g(2027)\).  Consequently \(g(2027)\) may be **any odd integer** (the
codomain of \(g\) is the set of odd integers).

Because \(g(2027)\) can be any odd integer, (10) shows that

\[
g(2025)=\text{any odd integer}.
\]

The required parity is satisfied automatically:
if \(g(2027)\) is odd, then \(g(2025)=g(2027)+6\) is also odd, and
\(g(-2025)=12-g(2027)\) is odd as well.

Finally, one can construct explicit examples (for instance,
\(g(n)=18n+9\) plus a suitable even‑valued “adjustment’’ function) that
realise any prescribed odd value of \(g(2025)\) while keeping the given
values \(g(0)=9,\;g(1)=27,\;g(2)=3,\;g(10)=63\).  Hence every odd integer
indeed occurs.

