--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  Reduction to a lattice problem  

Place the checkerboard vertices at the integer lattice \(\mathbb Z^{2}\).
Let the random square have centre \(c\) and orientation \(\theta\) (uniform in
\([0,2\pi)\)).  
Rotate the whole picture by \(-\theta\); the square becomes the fixed
axis‑aligned unit square \(Q\) centred at \(c' =R_{-\theta}c\) and the lattice
is rotated to  

\[
\Lambda_{\theta}=R_{-\theta}\mathbb Z^{2}
   =\{\, (a\cos\theta-b\sin\theta,\; a\sin\theta+b\cos\theta)\mid a,b\in\mathbb Z \,\}.
\]

Thus the problem is: for a fixed \(\theta\), choose the centre \(c'\) uniformly
in a fundamental parallelogram of \(\Lambda_{\theta}\) (area \(1\)).  
Let \(N\) be the number of points of \(\Lambda_{\theta}\) that lie inside the
square \(Q\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Only the cases \(N=0,1,2\) occur  

For a generic position the square can contain at most two lattice points; the
event \(N\ge 3\) has probability \(0\).  
Because the lattice has density \(1\),

\[
\mathbb E[N]=\text{area}(Q)\times\text{density}=1 .
\]

With \(p_0=\Pr(N=0),\;p_1=\Pr(N=1),\;p_2=\Pr(N=2)\),

\[
p_0+p_1+p_2=1,\qquad p_1+2p_2=1 .
\]

Solving gives \(p_0=p_2\). Hence

\[
p =\Pr(N=0)=\Pr(N=2).
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Probability of exactly two points  

Two points of \(\Lambda_{\theta}\) are of the form \(u\) and \(u+v\) where
\(v\neq0\) is an integer vector.  
For a fixed vector \(v=(a,b)\) the set of centres \(c'\) that make **both**
\(u\) and \(u+v\) lie in the square is the overlap of two unit squares
centred at those points.  Its area is  

\[
f_{\theta}(a,b)=\max\!\bigl(0,1-|\,a\cos\theta-b\sin\theta|\bigr)\,
                \max\!\bigl(0,1-|\,a\sin\theta+b\cos\theta|\bigr).
\]

A translation is uniformly distributed over a fundamental domain of area \(1\);
hence the probability that this particular unordered pair appears is
\(\tfrac12 f_{\theta}(a,b)\) (the factor \(\tfrac12\) avoids double‑counting
the pair \((u,u+v)\) and \((u+v,u)\)).  Summing over all non‑zero integer
vectors,

\[
p =\frac12\sum_{(a,b)\neq(0,0)}\; \frac1{2\pi}\int_{0}^{2\pi}
      f_{\theta}(a,b)\,d\theta .
\tag{1}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  Only vectors of length 1 contribute  

Write \(r=\sqrt{a^{2}+b^{2}}\).  
For a given \(\theta\) the vector
\((x,y)=R_{-\theta}(a,b)\) lies on the circle of radius \(r\).
The factors \((1-|x|)\) and \((1-|y|)\) are positive only when
\(|x|<1\) and \(|y|<1\); this requires the circle to intersect the interior
of the square \([-1,1]^2\).  
The circle of radius \(r\) meets that square only when \(r<1\); for
\(r=1\) it touches the square along a line segment, and for \(r>1\) it never
enters the interior.  Consequently

\[
f_{\theta}(a,b)=0\qquad\text{unless }a^{2}+b^{2}=1 .
\]

Thus only the four vectors \((\pm1,0),(0,\pm1)\) give a non‑zero contribution.

