        \[
\begin{aligned}
&\text{Let the circle be centred at }O(0,0),\; r=1.\\[2mm]
&\text{Choose a pair of opposite points }E,F\text{ on the circle.}\\
&\qquad E=(\cos\theta,\sin\theta),\;F=(-\cos\theta,-\sin\theta)=-E .
\end{aligned}
\]

For any point \(M\) on the boundary of the regular dodecagon,

\[
\begin{aligned}
\overrightarrow{MF}\cdot\overrightarrow{ME}
&=(F-M)\cdot(E-M)\\
&=F\!\cdot\!E-F\!\cdot\!M-M\!\cdot\!E+M\!\cdot\!M .
\end{aligned}
\]

Because \(F=-E\),

\[
F\!\cdot\!E = -E\!\cdot\!E = -1,\qquad
F+E = -E+E = 0 .
\]

Hence the mixed terms cancel and

\[
\overrightarrow{MF}\cdot\overrightarrow{ME}= -1+M\!\cdot\!M
=|M|^{2}-1 .
\]

Thus the dot product depends **only** on the distance of \(M\) from the centre; the direction of the diameter \((E,F)\) is irrelevant.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### Minimal distance of a point on a side

The regular dodecagon is inscribed in the unit circle, so its apothem (the distance from the centre to any side) is

\[
a = r\cos\frac{\pi}{12}= \cos\frac{\pi}{12}.
\]

For a given side, the point of the side closest to the centre is the foot of the perpendicular from \(O\); its distance from \(O\) is exactly the apothem \(a\).  
Consequently

\[
\min_{M\text{ on edges}}|M| = a = \cos\frac{\pi}{12}.
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### Minimum value of the dot product

\[
\begin{aligned}
S &= \min_{M,\;E,F} \overrightarrow{MF}\cdot\overrightarrow{ME}
   = \bigl(\min_{M}|M|^{2}\bigr)-1 \\
  &= \cos^{2}\frac{\pi}{12}-1
   = -\sin^{2}\frac{\pi}{12}.
\end{aligned}
\]

Now \(\displaystyle\sin\frac{\pi}{12}= \sin15^{\circ}
      =\frac{\sqrt6-\sqrt2}{4}\).

\[
\sin^{2}\frac{\pi}{12}= \left(\frac{\sqrt6-\sqrt2}{4}\right)^{2}
= \frac{8-4\sqrt3}{16}= \frac12-\frac{\sqrt3}{4}.
\]

Therefore

\[
S = -\Bigl(\frac12-\frac{\sqrt3}{4}\Bigr)
   = \frac{\sqrt3}{4}-\frac12 .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### Required integer

\[
-10000\,S = 10000\sin^{2}\frac{\pi}{12}
          = 10000\Bigl(\frac12-\frac{\sqrt3}{4}\Bigr)
          = 2500\,(2-\sqrt3).
\]

Numerically,
\[
2500\,(2-\sqrt3)\approx 669.872981\ldots
\]

Hence

\[
\boxed{\left\lceil -10000\,S\right\rceil = 670 }.
\]

