        Let  

* \(P\) be the regular \(2024\)-gon,  
* \(n\) distinct lines each intersecting the interior of \(P\) (a **suitable** line),  
* the lines together with the sides of \(P\) form a planar graph \(G\).

Denote  

* \(V\) – the vertices of \(G\) (original vertices of \(P\), the intersection points of two lines,
  and the intersection points of a line with a side of \(P\));
* \(E\) – the edges of \(G\);
* \(F\) – the faces of \(G\) that lie inside \(P\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  Counting vertices, edges and boundary edges  

*Every line meets the boundary of the convex polygon in exactly two points*,
hence the total number of line‑boundary intersection points is  

\[
I_{\partial}=2n .
\]

Consequently the boundary of \(P\) is cut into  

\[
B = 2024+I_{\partial}=2024+2n
\]

edges (these are the “boundary edges’’ of the graph).

Let  

\[
I_{\text{int}} =\text{ number of interior intersection points of two lines}.
\]

If a pair of lines intersect inside \(P\) we obtain one interior vertex; thus  

\[
V = 2024 + I_{\text{int}} + I_{\partial}=2024+I_{\text{int}}+2n .
\]

For a line that meets \(k\) other lines inside \(P\) the segment of the line
lying in the polygon is divided into \(k+1\) edges.
Summing over all lines gives  

\[
E_{\text{int}} = \sum_{i=1}^{n}(k_i+1)= n +\sum_{i=1}^{n}k_i
                = n + 2 I_{\text{int}} .
\]

Hence  

\[
E = E_{\text{int}}+B = n+2I_{\text{int}}+2024+2n
  = 2024+3n+2I_{\text{int}} .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Using Euler’s formula  

The graph \(G\) is connected (we may always choose the lines so that they
intersect each other) and its outer face is the region outside the polygon.
Euler’s formula gives  

\[
V-E+F = 2\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad 
F = E-V+2 .
\]

Only the faces inside the polygon are of interest, therefore  

\[
F_{\text{in}} = F-1 = E-V+1 .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  A lower bound for the total number of sides of the interior faces  

Every interior edge is incident with two interior faces, while each boundary
edge belongs to exactly one interior face.  Consequently  

\[
\sum_{f\text{ interior}} \! \operatorname{deg}(f)
      = 2E_{\text{int}} + B .
\]

If every interior face has at least five sides we obtain  

\[
2E_{\text{int}}+B \;\ge\; 5F_{\text{in}}
   = 5\bigl(E-V+1\bigr) .
\tag{1}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  Substituting the expressions  

From the previous section  

\[
\begin{aligned}
E_{\text{int}} &= n+2I_{\text{int}},\\
B &= 2024+2n,\\
E &= 2024+3n+2I_{\text{int}},\\
V &= 2024+2n+I_{\text{int}} .
\end{aligned}
\]

Insert these into (1) and simplify; the terms containing \(I_{\text{int}}\)
cancel:

\[
\begin{aligned}
2\bigl(n+2I_{\text{int}}\bigr)+2024+2n
   &\ge 5\bigl(2024+3n+2I_{\text{int}}
               -2024-2n-I_{\text{int}}+1\bigr)\\[2mm]
4n+2024 &\ge \frac52\,n(n+1)+5 .
\end{aligned}
\]

Multiplying by \(2\),

\[
4n+2024 \ge 5n^2+5n+10 .
\]

Re‑arranging gives the quadratic inequality  

\[
n^{2}+n-4038\le 0 .
\]

Its positive root is  

\[
n_{\max}= \frac{-1+\sqrt{1+4\cdot4038}}{2}
        =\frac{-1+\sqrt{16153}}{2}\approx 63.05 .
\]

Hence

\[
n\le 63\qquad\text{if all interior faces have at least five sides.}
\tag{2}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 5.  Showing that \(n=63\) can be attained  

Take three families of parallel lines, with slopes differing by \(60^{\circ}\)
(the directions of a regular hexagonal lattice).  
Let the numbers of lines in the three families be  

\[
a=21,\qquad b=21,\qquad c=21\qquad (a+b+c=63).
\]

The three families intersect pairwise, producing a regular hexagonal
tiling.  Every cell of the tiling is a hexagon; a cell that meets the
boundary of the large polygon loses one side and becomes a pentagon.
Because each side of the \(2024\)-gon is intersected by at least two lines
from each of the two families that are not parallel to that side,
no cell touches two consecutive sides of the polygon; consequently no
interior face is a triangle or a quadrilateral.

Thus an arrangement of \(63\) suitable lines exists for which **every**
smaller polygon has at least five sides.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 6.  Minimal number that forces a triangle or a quadrilateral  

Inequality (2) shows that with \(63\) lines it is still possible to avoid
triangles and quadrilaterals, while the construction above proves that this
is indeed possible.  Adding one more suitable line makes the inequality
impossible; therefore a triangle or a quadrilateral must appear.

\[
\boxed{\,n_{\min}=64\,}
\]

Hence at least **64** suitable lines are required to guarantee that among
the polygons obtained from a regular \(2024\)-gon there is a triangle or a
quadrilateral.

