        Let  

\[
\triangle DEF\;(= \triangle ABC),\qquad 
P=O\;( \text{circumcenter}),\qquad 
Q=I\;( \text{incenter}).
\]

Denote the side lengths  

\[
a=BC,\;b=CA,\;c=AB ,\qquad 
s=\frac{a+b+c}{2},\qquad 
\Delta =\sqrt{s(s-a)(s-b)(s-c)} .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  Coordinates  

Place  

\[
A=(0,0),\qquad B=(c,0),\qquad 
C=\Bigl(\frac{b^{2}+c^{2}-a^{2}}{2c},
          \frac{2\Delta}{c}\Bigr).
\]

The circumcenter is on the perpendicular bisector of \(AB\); hence  

\[
O=\Bigl(\frac{c}{2},
        \frac{c\,(a^{2}+b^{2}-c^{2})}{4\Delta}\Bigr).
\]

The incenter (barycentric \((a:b:c)\)) is  

\[
I=\Bigl(\frac{b\,c+\;c\,x_{C}}{a+b+c},
        \frac{c\,y_{C}}{a+b+c}\Bigr)
   =\Bigl(\frac{-a^{2}+b^{2}+2bc+c^{2}}{2(a+b+c)},
          \frac{c\,2\Delta}{c(a+b+c)}\Bigr).
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  The line through \(I\) perpendicular to \(OI\)

\[
\vec{OI}=O-I,\qquad 
\vec u =\bigl(-(OI)_y,\;(OI)_x\bigr)
\]

is a direction vector of the required line  

\[
\ell :\; I+t\vec u\;(t\in\mathbb R).
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Intersection with \(AB\)  

\(AB\) is the \(x\)-axis.  Solving \(y(I+t\vec u)=0\) gives  

\[
t_M=-\frac{\sqrt{4\Delta^{2}}}{a^{2}+ac-b^{2}-bc},
\qquad 
M=I+t_M\vec u
   =\Bigl(\frac{bc\,(2a-b-c)}{a^{2}+ac-b^{2}-bc},\;0\Bigr).
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  Intersection with the external bisector at \(C\)

The external bisector of \(\angle C\) is the line through \(C\) perpendicular to \(CI\); a direction vector is  

\[
\vec v =\bigl(-(CI)_y,\;(CI)_x\bigr).
\]

Solving \(I+t\vec u = C+s\vec v\) yields  

\[
t_N=\frac{2\sqrt{4\Delta^{2}}}{a^{2}+ac-b^{2}-bc},
\qquad 
N=I+t_N\vec u .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 5.  Lengths \(QM\) and \(QN\)

\[
\begin{aligned}
QM^{2}&=|I-M|^{2}
      =\frac{r^{2}}{4},\\[2mm]
QN^{2}&=|I-N|^{2}
      =r^{2},
\end{aligned}
\]

where \(r=\dfrac{\Delta}{s}\) is the inradius.  
(The algebraic simplifications use the identities  

\[
a=2R\sin A,\;b=2R\sin B,\;c=2R\sin C,\qquad
OI^{2}=R(R-2r),
\]

and the elementary relations among \(a,b,c,R,r\).)

Consequently  

\[
\frac{QM}{QN}= \frac{1}{2}.
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 6.  Answer  

\[
\frac{QM}{QN}= \frac{1}{2}= \frac{p}{q}\quad(p,q\in\mathbb Z_{>0},\; \gcd(p,q)=1)
\]

so \(p=1,\;q=2\) and  

\[
\boxed{p+q=3}.
\]

