        Let  

\[
P=(0,0),\qquad R=(d,0),\qquad Q=(x_{Q},y_{Q}),\qquad S=(x_{S},y_{S})
\]

with the quadrilateral taken in the order \(P,Q,R,S\) (so \(Q\) and \(S\) lie on opposite
sides of the line \(PR\)).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  What the coincidence of centres forces

For \(\triangle PQR\) let  

* \(O_{1}\) – its circumcenter,  
* \(I_{1}\) – its incenter.

For \(\triangle PRS\) let  

* \(O_{2}\) – its circumcenter,  
* \(I_{2}\) – its incenter.

The hypothesis is  

\[
O_{1}=I_{2},\qquad I_{1}=O_{2}.
\]

Both circumcentres lie on the perpendicular bisector of the common side \(PR\);
hence the two incentres also lie on this line.
Consequently the incenter of a triangle lies on the perpendicular bisector of a
side **iff** the triangle is isosceles with that side as its base.
Therefore

\[
\boxed{PQ=QR},\qquad\boxed{PS=RS}.
\]

Thus each triangle is isosceles with base \(PR\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Introducing the variables

Put  

\[
PR=d\;(0<d<2),\qquad
PQ=QR=1\;( \text{given}),\qquad
PS=RS=b .
\]

Because the triangles are symmetric about the line \(x=\frac d2\) we may write  

\[
Q\Bigl(\frac d2,\,h_{1}\Bigr),\qquad 
S\Bigl(\frac d2,\,-h_{2}\Bigr),
\]

with  

\[
h_{1}^{2}=1-\Bigl(\frac d2\Bigr)^{2},\qquad 
b^{2}= \Bigl(\frac d2\Bigr)^{2}+h_{2}^{2}\;(b>0).
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Radii and centres

For an isosceles triangle with base \(d\) and equal sides \(a\) the
inradius is  

\[
r=\frac{\Delta}{s}= \frac{d\,h}{d+2a},
\]

where \(h\) is the altitude and \(s\) the semiperimeter.
Hence  

\[
r_{1}= \frac{d\,h_{1}}{d+2},\qquad
r_{2}= \frac{d\,h_{2}}{d+2b}.
\]

The circumcentre of the same triangle lies on the same axis at  

\[
y_{c}= \frac{a^{2}-\bigl(\frac d2\bigr)^{2}}{2h}.
\]

Thus  

\[
y_{c}^{(1)}= \frac{h_{1}^{2}-\bigl(\frac d2\bigr)^{2}}{2h_{1}},\qquad
y_{c}^{(2)}= \frac{\bigl(\frac d2\bigr)^{2}-h_{2}^{2}}{2h_{2}} .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  Using the coincidences

The conditions \(O_{1}=I_{2}\) and \(I_{1}=O_{2}\) give

\[
\begin{cases}
r_{1}=y_{c}^{(2)}\\[2mm]
r_{2}= -\,y_{c}^{(1)} .
\end{cases}
\]

Substituting the expressions above and using \(h_{1}^{2}=1-(d/2)^{2}\) we obtain
the system

\[
\begin{aligned}
\frac{d\,h_{1}}{d+2}&=\frac{\bigl(\frac d2\bigr)^{2}-h_{2}^{2}}{2h_{2}},\\[2mm]
\frac{d\,h_{2}}{d+2b}&=\frac{\bigl(\frac d2\bigr)^{2}-h_{1}^{2}}{2h_{1}}
   =\frac{\frac{d^{2}}{2}-1}{2h_{1}} .
\end{aligned}
\]

Solving these equations (e.g. with a computer algebra system) yields the unique
positive solution

\[
d=\frac{1+\sqrt5}{2}\;(=\varphi),\qquad
h_{1}=h_{2}= \sin 36^{\circ},\qquad
b=1 .
\]

Hence all four sides of the quadrilateral are equal to \(1\); the figure is a
rhombus whose diagonals are perpendicular and whose longer diagonal is the
golden ratio \(\varphi\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 5.  Angle at \(P\)

Vectors  

\[
\overrightarrow{PQ}= \Bigl(\frac d2,\;h_{1}\Bigr),\qquad
\overrightarrow{PS}= \Bigl(\frac d2,\;-h_{2}\Bigr)
\]

have equal length \(1\).  Therefore

\[
\cos\angle P=
\frac{\overrightarrow{PQ}\cdot\overrightarrow{PS}}{1\cdot1}
   =\frac{\bigl(\frac d2\bigr)^{2}-h_{1}h_{2}}{1}
   =\frac{d^{2}}{4}-h_{1}^{2}.
\]

With \(d=\varphi\) and \(h_{1}= \sin36^{\circ}\),

\[
\cos\angle P =\frac{\varphi^{2}}{4}-\sin^{2}36^{\circ}
               =\frac{(1+\sqrt5)^{2}}{16}-\sin^{2}36^{\circ}
               =\frac{3+\sqrt5}{8}-\frac{5-\sqrt5}{8}
               =\frac{ \sqrt5-1}{4}= \cos 72^{\circ}.
\]

Thus  

\[
\boxed{\angle P = 72^{\circ}}.
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
The required angle at vertex \(P\) of the quadrilateral is \(72^{\circ}\).

