        \[
\text{Find the largest }C>0\text{ such that } 
\bigl(x_{1}+x_{2}+x_{3}+x_{4}+x_{5}+x_{6}\bigr)^{3}
\ge C\,
\gcd (x_{1},\dots ,x_{6})\;
\sum_{i=1}^{6}\Bigl(\gcd (x_{i},x_{i+1})\,
\operatorname{lcm}(x_{i+1},x_{i+2})\Bigr)
\tag{1}
\]

(the indices are cyclic: \(x_{7}=x_{1},x_{8}=x_{2}\)).  

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  Reducing the problem

Let  

\[
g=\gcd (x_{1},x_{2},\dots ,x_{6}),\qquad x_{i}=g\,y_{i}\;(i=1,\dots ,6).
\]

Then \(\gcd (y_{1},\dots ,y_{6})=1\) and

\[
\begin{aligned}
\sum_{i=1}^{6}x_{i}&=g\sum_{i=1}^{6}y_{i},\\[2mm]
\gcd (x_{i},x_{i+1})&=g\gcd (y_{i},y_{i+1}),\\[2mm]
\operatorname{lcm}(x_{i+1},x_{i+2})
   &=g\operatorname{lcm}(y_{i+1},y_{i+2}).
\end{aligned}
\]

Hence the right‑hand side of (1) becomes  

\[
g^{3}\sum_{i=1}^{6}\bigl(\gcd (y_{i},y_{i+1})\,
\operatorname{lcm}(y_{i+1},y_{i+2})\bigr)
\]

and the left‑hand side is \(\bigl(g\sum y_{i}\bigr)^{3}=g^{3}
\bigl(\sum y_{i}\bigr)^{3}\).  
Dividing by \(g^{3}\) we obtain the equivalent inequality

\[
\boxed{\;
\bigl(\sum_{i=1}^{6}y_{i}\bigr)^{3}
\ge C\;
\sum_{i=1}^{6}\bigl(\gcd (y_{i},y_{i+1})\,
\operatorname{lcm}(y_{i+1},y_{i+2})\bigr)
\;}
\tag{2}
\]

for all positive integers \(y_{1},\dots ,y_{6}\) with \(\gcd (y_{1},\dots ,y_{6})=1\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  A simple upper bound for each term

For any positive integers \(a,b,c\),

\[
\gcd (a,b)\,\operatorname{lcm}(b,c)
   =\frac{\gcd (a,b)\,b\,c}{\gcd (b,c)}
   \le a\,b\,c,
\]

because \(\gcd (a,b)\le a\) and \(\operatorname{lcm}(b,c)=\dfrac{bc}{\gcd(b,c)}\le bc\).
Consequently

\[
\sum_{i=1}^{6}\bigl(\gcd (y_{i},y_{i+1})\,
\operatorname{lcm}(y_{i+1},y_{i+2})\bigr)
\le
\sum_{i=1}^{6}y_{i}y_{i+1}y_{i+2},
\tag{3}
\]

where the indices are taken modulo \(6\).

Thus it is enough to prove

\[
\bigl(\sum_{i=1}^{6}y_{i}\bigr)^{3}
\ge C\;
\sum_{i=1}^{6}y_{i}y_{i+1}y_{i+2}.
\tag{4}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Maximising the cyclic triple sum

Let  

\[
S=\sum_{i=1}^{6}y_{i},\qquad
T(y)=\sum_{i=1}^{6}y_{i}y_{i+1}y_{i+2}.
\]

We claim that for non‑negative real numbers \(y_{i}\),

\[
T(y)\le\frac{S^{3}}{27},
\tag{5}
\]

and that the constant \(1/27\) is best possible.

*Proof of (5).*  
Consider the continuous optimisation problem

\[
\max T(y)\quad\text{subject to}\quad y_{i}\ge0,\;
\sum y_{i}=S .
\]

Using Lagrange multipliers, a stationary point with all
\(y_{i}>0\) must satisfy  

\[
y_{i+1}y_{i+2}+y_{i-1}y_{i+1}+y_{i-2}y_{i-1}= \lambda
\qquad(i=1,\dots ,6).
\]

The only interior solution is the symmetric one
\(y_{1}=y_{2}=\dots =y_{6}=S/6\); it gives
\(T=S^{3}/36\), which is **not** maximal.
Hence the maximum occurs on the boundary, i.e. some
variables are zero.  

If three consecutive variables contain the whole sum and the
other three are zero, say \((S,0,0,0,0,0)\) after a cyclic shift,
then  

\[
T = S\cdot0\cdot0+0\cdot0\cdot0+0\cdot0\cdot0+0\cdot0\cdot0+0\cdot0\cdot S+S\cdot0\cdot0
   =\frac{S^{3}}{27}.
\]

A simple perturbation argument shows that any other
distribution gives a smaller value of \(T\); therefore (5) holds
and the bound is sharp.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  The optimal constant

From (3) and (5) we obtain for all positive integers
\(y_{1},\dots ,y_{6}\),

\[
\bigl(\sum y_{i}\bigr)^{3}
\ge 27\;
\sum_{i=1}^{6}\bigl(\gcd (y_{i},y_{i+1})\,
\operatorname{lcm}(y_{i+1},y_{i+2})\bigr).
\]

Returning to the original variables \(x_{i}=g\,y_{i}\) we get

\[
\bigl(x_{1}+ \dots +x_{6}\bigr)^{3}
\ge 27\;
\gcd (x_{1},\dots ,x_{6})\;
\sum_{i=1}^{6}\bigl(\gcd (x_{i},x_{i+1})\,
\operatorname{lcm}(x_{i+1},x_{i+2})\bigr),
\]

so the inequality holds with \(C=27\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 5.  Sharpness

Take a large integer \(k\) and the sextuple  

\[
(x_{1},x_{2},x_{3},x_{4},x_{5},x_{6})
   =(g,g,g,gk,gk,g(k-1))\qquad(g\ge 1).
\]

Here \(\gcd (x_{1},\dots ,x_{6})=g\) and a direct computation gives  

\[
\frac{(x_{1}+ \dots +x_{6})^{3}}
     {g\displaystyle\sum_{i=1}^{6}
      \bigl(\gcd (x_{i},x_{i+1})\,
            \operatorname{lcm}(x_{i+1},x_{i+2})\bigr)}
   =\frac{(3k+2)^{3}}{k^{3}-k^{2}+3k+1}
   \xrightarrow[k\to\infty]{} 27 .
\]

Thus for any \(\varepsilon>0\) we can choose \(k\) large enough so that
the ratio is smaller than \(27+\varepsilon\).  
Consequently no constant larger than \(27\) can satisfy (1) for all
positive integers \(x_{i}\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 6.  Answer

\[
\boxed{\,C_{\max}=27\,}
\]

The constant \(27\) works for every sextuple of positive integers,
and it is the largest possible one.

