        \[
\text{Find the largest }C>0\text{ such that for all }x_{1},\dots ,x_{6}\in \mathbb Z_{>0}
\]
\[
\bigl(x_{1}+x_{2}+x_{3}+x_{4}+x_{5}+x_{6}\bigr)^{3}
\ge C\,
\gcd (x_{1},\dots ,x_{6})\;
\sum_{i=1}^{6}\Bigl(\gcd (x_{i},x_{i+1})\,
\operatorname{lcm}(x_{i+1},x_{i+2})\Bigr) .
\tag{1}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  Homogeneity  

Both sides of (1) are homogeneous of degree 3:  
if all numbers are multiplied by a positive integer \(t\) then the left–hand side
becomes \(t^{3}\) times larger and the right–hand side also becomes \(t^{3}\) times larger.
Hence we may divide the six numbers by their common divisor and assume  

\[
g:=\gcd (x_{1},\dots ,x_{6})=1 .
\tag{2}
\]

With this reduction (1) is equivalent to  

\[
\bigl(x_{1}+x_{2}+x_{3}+x_{4}+x_{5}+x_{6}\bigr)^{3}
\ge C\,
\sum_{i=1}^{6}\Bigl(\gcd (x_{i},x_{i+1})\,
\operatorname{lcm}(x_{i+1},x_{i+2})\Bigr) .
\tag{3}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  A simple upper bound for each summand  

For any positive integers \(a,b,c\)

\[
\gcd (a,b)\,\operatorname{lcm}(b,c)
   =\gcd (a,b)\,\frac{bc}{\gcd (b,c)}
   \le a\cdot b\cdot c ,
\]

because \(\gcd (a,b)\le a\) and \(\gcd (b,c)\ge 1\).
Consequently, for the six‑tuple (3)

\[
\sum_{i=1}^{6}\Bigl(\gcd (x_{i},x_{i+1})\,
\operatorname{lcm}(x_{i+1},x_{i+2})\Bigr)
\le
\sum_{i=1}^{6}x_{i}x_{i+1}x_{i+2}.
\tag{4}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Bounding the cyclic triple product  

Set  

\[
S=x_{1}+x_{2}+x_{3}+x_{4}+x_{5}+x_{6}.
\]

We claim  

\[
\sum_{i=1}^{6}x_{i}x_{i+1}x_{i+2}\le\frac{S^{3}}{27}.
\tag{5}
\]

*Proof of (5).*  
Consider the continuous optimisation problem  

\[
\max\;F(x_{1},\dots ,x_{6})
   =\sum_{i=1}^{6}x_{i}x_{i+1}x_{i+2}
\quad\text{subject to}\quad
x_{i}\ge 0,\;\sum_{i=1}^{6}x_{i}=S .
\]

\(F\) is a homogeneous polynomial of degree 3; therefore we may fix \(S=1\).
Using Lagrange multipliers, for a stationary point we must have  

\[
x_{i-2}x_{i-1}+x_{i-1}x_{i+1}+x_{i+1}x_{i+2}= \lambda
\qquad(i=1,\dots ,6),
\tag{6}
\]

where the indices are taken modulo 6.
If at a stationary point four consecutive variables are positive,
subtracting the equations for two adjacent indices shows that the
outer two of them must be equal; repeating the argument forces at most
three consecutive variables to be positive.
Hence a maximiser can have at most three consecutive non‑zero entries.
Assume \(x_{1}=x_{2}=x_{3}=t\) and \(x_{4}=x_{5}=x_{6}=0\) (any cyclic
translation gives the same value).  
The constraint gives \(3t=S\), i.e. \(t=S/3\), and

\[
F_{\max}=t^{3}= \Bigl(\frac{S}{3}\Bigr)^{3}= \frac{S^{3}}{27}.
\]

Thus (5) holds for all non‑negative reals, and a fortiori for positive
integers.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  The inequality with the constant \(C=27\)

Combining (4) and (5) we obtain, under the assumption (2),

\[
\sum_{i=1}^{6}\Bigl(\gcd (x_{i},x_{i+1})\,
\operatorname{lcm}(x_{i+1},x_{i+2})\Bigr)
\le \frac{S^{3}}{27},
\]

