        **Answer**

\[
\boxed{|S|_{\min}=17\qquad\text{and}\qquad |T|_{\min}=2 } 
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### Why at least 17 integers are needed for \(S\)

For a set \(S\) with \(|S|=n\) the set of (positive) differences  

\[
\Delta(S)=\{x-y\;:\;x,y\in S,\;x>y\}
\]

contains at most  

\[
\binom{n}{2}= \frac{n(n-1)}{2}
\]

different numbers, because each unordered pair \(\{x,y\}\) contributes a single
positive difference.  

In the problem we must realise the 124 Fibonacci numbers  

\[
F_{2},F_{3},\dots ,F_{125}
\]

and, in addition, the Lucas number \(L_{100}\); altogether we need  

\[
m=125
\]

different positive differences.  
Hence we must have  

\[
\binom{n}{2}\ge m\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad 
\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\ge125 .
\]

Solving the quadratic inequality gives  

\[
n\ge\frac{1+\sqrt{1+8\cdot125}}{2}
      =\frac{1+\sqrt{1001}}{2}\approx16.32 .
\]

Thus any such set must contain at least  

\[
n_{\min}=17
\]

integers.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### Existence of a set with 17 integers

A set of 17 integers whose difference set contains **any** prescribed set of
125 positive integers always exists.  
Indeed, a set of \(n\) points on a line whose consecutive gaps are chosen
appropriately yields a *Golomb ruler* of order \(n\); the distances between the
points are all distinct, giving exactly \(\binom{n}{2}=136\) different positive
differences.  

Because \(\binom{17}{2}=136>125\), we can assign each required number
\(F_{2},\dots ,F_{125},L_{100}\) to a distinct unordered pair of the 17
points.  The distances of a Golomb ruler can be chosen freely (they only have to
be distinct), so we may select the gaps so that the 125 prescribed numbers
appear among those 136 distances.  Consequently a set \(S\) with \(|S|=17\)
satisfying the required condition exists.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### The second condition

For a single prescribed non‑zero difference (here \(L_{100}\)) the smallest
possible set is simply two integers, e.g. \(\{0,L_{100}\}\).  Hence

\[
|T|_{\min}=2 .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------

Therefore the smallest possible cardinalities are  

\[
\boxed{|S|=17\quad\text{and}\quad|T|=2 } .
\]

