        The Fibonacci numbers are  

\[
F_{0}=0,\;F_{1}=1,\;F_{n+1}=F_{n}+F_{n-1}\qquad (n\ge 1)
\]

and the Lucas numbers satisfy  

\[
L_{0}=2,\;L_{1}=1,\;L_{n+1}=L_{n}+L_{n-1}\qquad (n\ge 1),\qquad   
L_{n}=F_{n-1}+F_{n+1}.
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  A set that works

For every integer \(k\ge 2\)

\[
F_{k}=F_{k+1}-F_{k-1}\qquad\text{(since }F_{k+1}=F_{k}+F_{k-1}\text{)} .
\]

Hence every *odd*‑indexed Fibonacci number is the difference of two
consecutive *even*‑indexed Fibonacci numbers.
Consequently, if a set contains **all even‑indexed Fibonacci numbers**
(and also the number \(0\)), then

* each even‑indexed Fibonacci number appears as a difference with \(0\);
* each odd‑indexed Fibonacci number appears as a difference of two
consecutive even‑indexed numbers.

Thus the set  

\[
S=\{F_{2t}\;|\;0\le t\le 63\}
   =\{0,1,3,8,21,55,\dots ,F_{126}\}
\]

has the required property.
The set contains the even‑indexed Fibonacci numbers from \(F_{0}\) up to
\(F_{126}\); there are  

\[
\frac{126-0}{2}+1=64
\]

elements, so \(|S|\le 64\).

Moreover  

\[
L_{100}=F_{101}+F_{99}=F_{102}-F_{98},
\]

so \(L_{100}\) is also a difference of two even‑indexed Fibonacci
numbers; therefore the same set \(S\) also provides a pair
\(a,b\in S\) with \(a-b=L_{100}\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Why 64 is minimal

Assume a set \(S\) of integers satisfies the condition.
Let \(m\) be the smallest even index such that \(F_{2m}\notin S\).
Consider the required difference \(F_{2m}\); it must be realised as
\(x-y\) with \(x>y\) and \(x,y\in S\).

Write the Zeckendorf representation of the two numbers:
every positive integer can be written uniquely as a sum of non‑consecutive
Fibonacci numbers.
If neither \(x\) nor \(y\) contains the term \(F_{2m}\) in its
Zeckendorf expansion, then the expansion of \(x-y\) would contain at least
two distinct Fibonacci summands, contradicting the fact that
\(x-y=F_{2m}\) is itself a single Fibonacci number.
Hence one of the two numbers must be exactly \(F_{2m}\) (or \(0\)).
Because the set must also contain \(0\) (otherwise the smallest required
difference \(F_{2}=1\) could not be obtained), the only way to obtain
\(F_{2m}\) is to have the element \(F_{2m}\) itself in the set.

Thus **every even‑indexed Fibonacci number up to \(F_{126}\) must belong to
\(S\)**.  There are exactly 64 such numbers (including \(F_{0}=0\)).
Consequently \(|S|\ge 64\).

Combined with the construction above we obtain  

\[
\boxed{|S|=64}.
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  The second set

To obtain the difference \(L_{100}\) it suffices to take any two
integers whose difference equals that number; for instance  

\[
T=\{0,\;L_{100}\}
\]

works, and no smaller set can contain two distinct integers.
Hence  

\[
\boxed{|T|=2}.
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
**Answer**

* the smallest possible cardinality of a set \(S\) with the required
property is \(\displaystyle 64\);
* the smallest possible cardinality of a set \(T\) with a pair whose
difference equals \(L_{100}\) is \(\displaystyle 2\).

