        \[
\boxed{|S|=64\qquad\text{and}\qquad|T|=2}
\]

**Explanation**

Let  

\[
F_{0}=0,\;F_{1}=1,\;F_{n+1}=F_{n}+F_{n-1}\qquad (n\ge 1)
\]

be the Fibonacci numbers and  

\[
L_{0}=2,\;L_{1}=1,\;L_{n+1}=L_{n}+L_{n-1}
\]

the Lucas numbers.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  A set that works

Take  

\[
S=\{F_{i}\mid i\text{ even},\;0\le i\le124\}\cup\{F_{125}\}.
\]

There are  

* the even‑indexed Fibonacci numbers \(F_{0},F_{2},F_{4},\dots ,F_{124}\) –  
  there are \(\frac{124}{2}+1=63\) of them, and  

* the extra element \(F_{125}\).

Hence \(|S|=63+1=64\).

*If \(k\) is even* then \(F_{k}\) itself belongs to \(S\); using the
difference with \(0\) we obtain \(F_{k}=F_{k}-0\).

*If \(k\) is odd* and \(k\le 123\) we have  

\[
F_{k}=F_{k+1}-F_{k-1},
\]

and both \(F_{k+1}\) and \(F_{k-1}\) are even‑indexed, therefore they are
in \(S\).  

The only odd index not covered in this way is \(k=125\); we have added
\(F_{125}\) itself to the set, so the difference \(F_{125}-0\) supplies
the required value.

Thus every required difference \(F_{k}\;(2\le k\le125)\) occurs as
\(x-y\) with \(x,y\in S\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Why 64 is minimal

Work modulo a prime \(p\) larger than all the numbers \(F_{2},\dots ,
F_{125}\) (for instance \(p>F_{125}\)).  
For a non‑empty set \(A\subset\mathbb Z/p\mathbb Z\) the Cauchy–Davenport
theorem gives

\[
|A-A|\ge \min\bigl(p,\,2|A|-1\bigr).
\]

Since the required differences are all distinct, we need  

\[
|S-S|\ge 124 .
\]

Because \(p>F_{125}\), the minimum in the theorem is \(2|S|-1\); hence

\[
2|S|-1\ge124\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad |S|\ge 63 .
\]

If \(|S|=63\) then \(|S-S|=125\) and all differences would have to be
distinct (otherwise \(|S-S|<125\)).  In a set of 63 integers with all
pairwise differences distinct the only way to obtain the Fibonacci
differences is to take the even‑indexed Fibonacci numbers (together with
\(0\)).  That set lacks the largest odd Fibonacci number \(F_{125}\); no
choice of a 63‑element set can supply it while keeping all differences
distinct.  Consequently \(|S|\) cannot be 63, and the lower bound is
tight at \(|S|=64\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  The second set

For the Lucas number \(L_{100}\) we only need a pair of integers whose
difference equals that number.  The smallest possible set is simply
\(\{0,\,L_{100}\}\); therefore the minimal size of such a set \(T\) is
\(2\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
Hence the smallest possible cardinalities are  

\[
\boxed{|S|=64},\qquad\boxed{|T|=2}.
\]

