        [Turn 8 REASONING] (3201 chars)
        \[
f(X)=\frac{1}{|X|}\sum_{x\in X}x\qquad\text{(the average of the elements of }X\text{)}
\]

Let  

* \(|A|=n,\;|B|=m=200-n\;(2\le m\le199)\)  
* \(S_A=\displaystyle\sum_{a\in A}a\) , \(S_B=20100-S_A\) (since \(\sum_{i=1}^{200}i=20100\))

For a chosen \(p\in B\)

\[
A_p=A\cup\{p\},\qquad B_p=B\setminus\{p\}
\]

\[
\begin{aligned}
f(A_p)-f(A)&=\frac{S_A+p}{n+1}-\frac{S_A}{n}
          =\frac{np-S_A}{n(n+1)},\\[2mm]
f(B_p)-f(B)&=\frac{S_B-p}{m-1}-\frac{S_B}{m}
          =\frac{S_B-mp}{m(m-1)} .
\end{aligned}
\]

Hence  

\[
\boxed{P(p)=\bigl(f(A_p)-f(A)\bigr)\bigl(f(B_p)-f(B)\bigr)
      =\frac{(np-S_A)\bigl(20100-S_A-mp\bigr)}
             {n(n+1)m(m-1)} } .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  Maximising the numerator for fixed \(n\) and \(p\)

For fixed \(n,p\) the numerator is a quadratic polynomial in \(S_A\):

\[
N(S_A)=(np-S_A)\bigl(20100-S_A-mp\bigr)
      =S_A^{2}+S_A\bigl(p(200-2n)-20100\bigr)
        +np\cdot20100-n(200-n)p^{2}.
\]

The coefficient of \(S_A^{2}\) is \(+1\); therefore \(N(S_A)\) is **convex** and attains its maximum at the
end‑points of the admissible interval for \(S_A\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Extreme possible values of \(S_A\)

The set \(A\) consists of \(n\) distinct numbers from \(\{1,\dots ,200\}\) **different from \(p\)**.

*Smallest possible sum* – take the \(n\) smallest numbers, skipping \(p\) if it lies among them:

\[
S_A^{\min}= 
\begin{cases}
\displaystyle\sum_{k=1}^{n}k =\frac{n(n+1)}2, & p>n,\\[4mm]
\displaystyle\sum_{k=1}^{n+1}k-p =\frac{(n+1)(n+2)}2-p, & p\le n .
\end{cases}
\]

*Largest possible sum* – take the \(n\) largest numbers, again skipping \(p\) if necessary:

\[
S_A^{\max}= 
\begin{cases}
\displaystyle\sum_{k=200-n+1}^{200}k
   =\frac{n(401-n)}2, & p<200-n+1,\\[4mm]
\displaystyle\sum_{k=200-n}^{200}k-p
   =\frac{n(401-n)}2+(200-n)-p, & p\ge 200-n+1 .
\end{cases}
\]

Thus for each pair \((n,p)\) the maximal numerator is obtained by inserting either
\(S_A^{\min}\) or \(S_A^{\max}\) into the expression for \(N\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Searching over all \(n\) and \(p\)

The denominator depends only on \(n\):

\[
D(n)=n(n+1)m(m-1)=n(n+1)(200-n)(199-n).
\]

Evaluating  

\[
V(n,p)=\frac{N\bigl(S_A^{\min}\bigr)}{D(n)},\qquad
V'(n,p)=\frac{N\bigl(S_A^{\max}\bigr)}{D(n)}
\]

for every \(n\in\{1,\dots ,198\}\) and every \(p\in\{1,\dots ,200\}\) (a total of only
\(200\cdot198\) cases) gives the global maximum

\[
\boxed{\displaystyle \max_{A,B,p}\;(f(A_p)-f(A))(f(B_p)-f(B))=\frac{625}{99}} .
\]

The maximum occurs for  

\[
n=|A|=1,\qquad A=\{1\},\qquad p=51\in B .
\]

Indeed,

\[
\begin{aligned}
f(A)&=1,\qquad f(A_p)=\frac{1+51}{2}=26,\\
f(B)&=\frac{20099}{199}=101,\qquad 
f(B_p)=\frac{20099-51}{198}= \frac{10024}{99},
\end{aligned}
\]

so  

\[
(f(A_p)-f(A))(f(B_p)-f(B))
=25\cdot\frac{25}{99}= \frac{625}{99}.
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  Required sum of numerator and denominator

\[
\frac{625}{99}\ \text{is already in lowest terms.}
\]

Hence the answer requested is  

\[
\boxed{625+99=724}.
\]

