        \[
f(X)=\frac{1}{|X|}\sum_{x\in X}x\qquad\text{(average of the elements of }X\text{)}
\]

Let  

* \(|A|=a,\;|B|=b=200-a\)  
* \(S_A=\sum_{x\in A}x,\;S_B=\sum_{x\in B}x\;(S_A+S_B=20100)\)  

For \(p\in B\)

\[
\begin{aligned}
f(A_p)-f(A)&=\frac{S_A+p}{a+1}-\frac{S_A}{a}
          =\frac{ap-S_A}{a(a+1)}
          =\frac{p-f(A)}{a+1},\\[2mm]
f(B_p)-f(B)&=\frac{S_B-p}{b-1}-\frac{S_B}{b}
          =\frac{S_B-bp}{b(b-1)}
          =\frac{f(B)-p}{b-1}.
\end{aligned}
\]

Hence  

\[
\boxed{P:=\bigl(f(A_p)-f(A)\bigr)\bigl(f(B_p)-f(B)\bigr)
      =\frac{(p-f(A))(f(B)-p)}{(a+1)(b-1)}} .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  Maximising the numerator

For a fixed \(a\) the averages satisfy  

\[
f(B)-f(A)=\frac{S_B}{b}-\frac{S_A}{a}
        =\frac{20100-a\,f(A)}{b}-f(A)
        =100-\frac{2}{b}\bigl(S_A-a\cdot100.5\bigr).
\]

The largest possible difference occurs when \(A\) consists of the
\(a\) smallest (or the \(a\) largest) numbers; then  

\[
f(A)=\frac{a+1}{2},\qquad f(B)=\frac{a+201}{2},
\]
so that  

\[
f(B)-f(A)=100 .
\]

Thus the numerator \((p-f(A))(f(B)-p)\) is a concave quadratic in \(p\)
with maximum \(\displaystyle\frac{(f(B)-f(A))^{2}}{4}=2500\),
attained at the midpoint  

\[
c=\frac{f(A)+f(B)}2=\frac{a+101}{2}.
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Choosing an admissible integer \(p\)

*If \(a\le 99\)*, the interval \(B=\{a+1,\dots ,200\}\) contains the
midpoint \(c\).  

  * When \(a\) is odd, \(c\) is an integer, so we can take \(p=c\) and
    obtain the numerator \(2500\).

  * When \(a\) is even, \(c\) is a half‑integer; the nearest integers are
    \(c\pm\frac12\).  The numerator then equals
    \(\displaystyle2500-\frac14=\frac{9999}{4}\).

*If \(a\ge100\)*, the midpoint lies **below** the smallest element of
\(B\); the closest admissible element is \(p=a+1\).  In this case

\[
(p-f(A))(f(B)-p)=\frac{(a+1)(199-a)}{4},
\]

and the product simplifies to \(\displaystyle\frac14\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  The product for each \(a\)

\[
P_{\text{max}}(a)=
\begin{cases}
\displaystyle\frac{2500}{(a+1)(199-a)}, & a\le 99,\ a\ \text{odd},\\[6pt]
\displaystyle\frac{9999}{4\,(a+1)(199-a)}, & a\le 98,\ a\ \text{even},\\[6pt]
\displaystyle\frac14, & a\ge 100 .
\end{cases}
\]

The denominator \((a+1)(199-a)\) is smallest when \(a\) is smallest.
Hence the overall maximum occurs at \(a=1\) (odd) :

\[
P_{\max}= \frac{2500}{(1+1)(199-1)}=\frac{2500}{396}
        =\frac{625}{99}.
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  A concrete attaining configuration

Take  

\[
A=\{1\},\qquad B=\{2,3,\dots ,200\},\qquad p=51\in B .
\]

Then  

\[
f(A)=1,\; f(A_p)=\frac{1+51}{2}=26,\qquad
f(B)=101,\; f(B_p)=\frac{20048}{198}=101+\frac{25}{99},
\]

so  

\[
\bigl(f(A_p)-f(A)\bigr)\bigl(f(B_p)-f(B)\bigr)
=25\cdot\frac{25}{99}= \frac{625}{99}.
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 5.  Required sum

The fraction \(\frac{625}{99}\) is already in lowest terms.
Hence the required sum of numerator and denominator is  

\[
\boxed{625+99=724}.
\]

