I think this is a known problem about "Soddy spheres" where the ratio of outer to inner radii is constant for a configuration where the outer sphere is not uniquely identifiable because the set of five spheres is "self-dual". That is, the set of five spheres is such that each sphere can be considered as the outer sphere for the other four. This property would make it impossible to determine which is the outer sphere. This is known as a "Soddy sphere packing" where the curvatures satisfy a certain quadratic equation that yields a set of five curvatures closed under the Descartes transformation. This is analogous to the "Apollonian gasket" in 2D where you have four mutually tangent circles, and you can replace any circle with the other solution, generating an infinite packing. In 3D, with five mutually tangent spheres, you can replace any sphere with its "dual". If you have a configuration where the set of five spheres is invariant under this replacement (i.e., each sphere is the dual of another sphere in the set), then the set is "self-dual". In such a configuration, you cannot tell which sphere is the outer one because each sphere could be considered outer relative to the other four.

Thus we need to find a set of five curvatures {b1,...,b5} such that for each i, the curvature b_i is the "dual" of some other curvature in the set. The dual curvature for sphere i given the other four is given by the Descartes formula: if we have curvatures b1,...,b5, then for each i, the other four curvatures satisfy the Descartes relation, and the two possible solutions for the fifth curvature are b_i and b_i' (the dual). So we need b_i' to be equal to some b_j in the set (maybe j ≠ i). This yields a closed set under the transformation.

Thus we need a set of five curvatures closed under the operation that replaces a curvature with the other solution of the Descartes equation for the other four. This is analogous to a "Soddy sphere" configuration where the curvatures form a "Soddy quintet".

