        \[
\begin{aligned}
&\triangle DEF:\quad \angle F=90^\circ ,\; \angle D=30^\circ ,\; EF=1 \\[2mm]
&\Rightarrow\; DE=2,\; DF=\sqrt3 .
\end{aligned}
\]

Place  

\[
F=(0,0),\qquad D=(\sqrt3,0),\qquad E=(0,1).
\]

A point on each side can be written with parameters  

\[
\begin{aligned}
P&\in DE :\;P\bigl(\sqrt3(1-t),\,t\bigr), &&t\in[0,1],\\
Q&\in EF :\;Q\bigl(0,\,1-s\bigr), &&s\in[0,1],\\
R&\in FD :\;R\bigl(\sqrt3\,u,\,0\bigr), &&u\in[0,1].
\end{aligned}
\]

The three side‑length squares of \(\triangle PQR\) are  

\[
\begin{aligned}
d_1^2&=|PQ|^2=3(1-t)^2+(t+s-1)^2,\\
d_2^2&=|QR|^2=3u^2+(s-1)^2,\\
d_3^2&=|RP|^2=3(1-t-u)^2+t^2 .
\end{aligned}
\]

Let  

\[
M=\max\{d_1,d_2,d_3\}.
\]

To minimise \(M\) we may assume at the optimum the three distances are equal;
otherwise the longest one could be reduced by moving the corresponding vertex.
Hence we set  

\[
d_1^2=d_2^2=d_3^2 .
\]

Solving  

\[
\begin{cases}
3(1-t)^2+(t+s-1)^2=3u^2+(s-1)^2,\\[2mm]
3u^2+(s-1)^2=3(1-t-u)^2+t^2,
\end{cases}
\]

gives the linear relations  

\[
u=2t-1,\qquad s=4t-2 .
\]

Because \(t,s,u\in[0,1]\) we obtain  

\[
\frac12\le t\le\frac34 .
\]

With these relations the common squared length becomes  

\[
M^2=3u^2+(s-1)^2=28t^{2}-36t+12 .
\]

Minimising on \([\tfrac12,\tfrac34]\),

\[
\frac{d}{dt}(28t^{2}-36t+12)=56t-36=0\Longrightarrow t=\frac{9}{14},
\]

which indeed lies in the allowed interval.  Substituting \(t=\frac{9}{14}\),

\[
M^{2}=28\Bigl(\frac{9}{14}\Bigr)^{2}-36\Bigl(\frac{9}{14}\Bigr)+12
      =\frac{3}{7}.
\]

Thus the smallest possible value of the longest side is  

\[
M=\sqrt{\frac{3}{7}} .
\]

The corresponding points are  

\[
t=\frac{9}{14},\qquad s=4t-2=\frac{4}{7},\qquad u=2t-1=\frac{2}{7},
\]

which are interior points of the three sides, so the configuration is admissible.

Hence the answer is \(\displaystyle \sqrt{\frac{3}{7}}\); in lowest terms \(a=3,\;b=7\).

\[
\boxed{a+b=10}
\]

