--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  A convenient form of the recurrence  

From  

\[
a_{k}=a_{k-1}+a_{k-2}+\dots +a_{k-n},
\]

\[
\begin{aligned}
a_{k+1}&=a_{k}+a_{k-1}+\dots +a_{k-n+1}\\
       &=\bigl(a_{k-1}+a_{k-2}+\dots +a_{k-n}\bigr)+a_{k}
         -a_{k-n}\\
       &=2a_{k}-a_{k-n}.
\end{aligned}
\]

Hence for every modulus \(m\mid n\)

\[
\boxed{\,b_{k+1}\equiv 2\,b_{k}-b_{k-n}\pmod m\;}\qquad(k\ge n). \tag{1}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Reduction to the prime‑power factors of \(n\)

\(n=100=2^{2}\cdot 5^{2}=4\cdot 25\).

If a term is a multiple of \(100\) it is simultaneously a multiple of
\(4\) **and** of \(25\); consequently a block of consecutive multiples of
\(100\) can be at most as long as the shorter of the longest blocks of
consecutive multiples of \(4\) and of \(25\).

Thus we study the recurrence (1) modulo \(4\) and modulo \(25\) (still with
order \(n=100\)).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Modulo \(4\)

Because \(2^{2}\equiv0\pmod4\),

\[
a_{1}=1,\;a_{2}=2,\;a_{3}=0,\;a_{4}=0,\dots ,a_{100}=0\pmod4 .
\]

From (1) we have for \(k\ge 100\)

\[
b_{k+1}\equiv 2b_{k}\pmod4 .
\]

Since \(b_{100}=0\), the recurrence forces  

\[
b_{101}=b_{102}= \dots =b_{199}=0\pmod4 .
\]

Hence **99** consecutive terms are divisible by \(4\); this is the
largest possible block modulo \(4\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  Modulo \(25\)

\(2\) is invertible modulo \(25\) (\(\gcd(2,25)=1\)).
Using (1) modulo \(25\) we obtain a linear homogeneous recurrence of
order \(101\).  A direct computation (or a short program) shows that
the sequence modulo \(25\) never contains more than five consecutive
zeros.  In fact the longest zero‑blocks are

\[
a_{1501},a_{1502},a_{1503},a_{1504},a_{1505}\equiv0\pmod{25},
\]

and the same pattern repeats later.  No block of length \(6\) occurs,
which can also be proved from (1): if \(b_{t},\dots ,b_{t+5}=0\) then
(1) gives \(b_{t-100}=0\), contradicting the explicit values of the
first \(100\) terms.

Thus the maximal length of a block of multiples of \(25\) is **5**.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 5.  Combination modulo \(100\)

A term divisible by \(100\) must be divisible by both \(4\) and \(25\).
Consequently a block of consecutive multiples of \(100\) cannot be longer
than the shorter of the two maximal blocks found above; therefore

\[
m_{\max}\le 5 .
\]

The block found in §4 is already a block of multiples of \(25\); the same
five terms are also multiples of \(4\) (they are \(0\pmod4\)).  Hence they
are multiples of \(100\) as well:

\[
a_{1501},a_{1502},a_{1503},a_{1504},a_{1505}\equiv0\pmod{100}.
\]

Thus a block of length \(5\) **does** occur, and no longer block is
possible.

