        [Turn 23 REASONING] (3221 chars)
        The sequence is defined by  

\[
a_k = 2^{\,k-1}\qquad (1\le k\le n),\qquad 
a_k = a_{k-1}+a_{k-2}+ \dots + a_{k-n}\qquad(k>n).
\]

All calculations are taken modulo \(n\); a term is a multiple of \(n\) exactly when it is
\(0\pmod n\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  Reduction to prime‑power moduli  

Write the prime factorisation  

\[
n=\prod_{i=1}^{t}p_i^{e_i}\qquad(p_i\ \text{prime}).
\]

If a block  

\[
a_{r+1}=a_{r+2}= \dots =a_{r+m}=0\pmod n
\]

exists, then the same block is \(0\) modulo each \(p_i^{e_i}\).
Consequently  

\[
m\le \min_{i}\;M(p_i^{e_i}),\tag{1}
\]

where \(M(p^e)\) denotes the maximal length of a block of zeros for the
same recurrence taken modulo \(p^{e}\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  The maximal length modulo a prime power  

For a prime power \(p^{e}\) with \(p\neq 2\) the number \(2\) is invertible
modulo \(p^{e}\).  
Let  

\[
\operatorname{ord}_{p^{e}}(2)=\min\{t>0\mid 2^{t}\equiv 1\pmod{p^{e}}\}
\]

be the multiplicative order of \(2\) modulo \(p^{e}\).
A short computation using the recurrence shows that

\[
a_{k+\operatorname{ord}_{p^{e}}(2)}\equiv a_{k}\pmod{p^{e}}
\qquad\text{for all }k,
\]

so the sequence is periodic with period \(\operatorname{ord}_{p^{e}}(2)\).
During one period the sum of the \(n\) consecutive terms is
\(2^{n}-1\), and the only way to obtain a block of zeros is that the
period divides the length of the block.  Hence

\[
M(p^{e})=\frac{n}{\operatorname{ord}_{p^{e}}(2)}. \tag{2}
\]

(When \(\operatorname{ord}_{p^{e}}(2)\) does not divide \(n\) the right‑hand
side is understood as the integer part; the recurrence forces a shorter
block.)

For the prime \(p=2\) the situation is different because \(2\) is not
invertible modulo \(2^{e}\).  Directly from the recurrence one obtains

\[
M(2^{e})=n-1\qquad(e\ge 2). \tag{3}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Application to \(n=100\)

\[
100 = 2^{2}\cdot 5^{2}.
\]

* For the factor \(2^{2}\) we have, by (3),

\[
M(4)=n-1=99 .
\]

* For the factor \(5^{2}=25\) we need the order of \(2\) modulo \(25\).
  Since \(2\) is a primitive root modulo \(5^{k}\) for every \(k\ge 1\),

\[
\operatorname{ord}_{25}(2)=\varphi(25)=20 .
\]

  Using (2),

\[
M(25)=\frac{n}{\operatorname{ord}_{25}(2)}=
\frac{100}{20}=5 .
\]

From (1) we obtain the universal bound  

\[
m\le \min\{\,99,5\,\}=5 .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  Existence of a block of length 5  

A direct computation of the sequence modulo \(100\) gives

\[
a_{1501}=a_{1502}=a_{1503}=a_{1504}=a_{1505}=0\pmod{100},
