        Let the diagonals \(WY\) and \(XZ\) intersect at \(M\).  
Put \(M\) at the origin and let the two diagonals be the lines

\[
WY:\;y=0,\qquad XZ:\;y=\tan\theta\,x
\]

where \(\theta\) is the angle between the diagonals.

Write the vertices as  

\[
W=(\alpha,0),\;Y=(-\beta,0),\qquad 
X=\gamma(\cos\theta,\sin\theta),\;Z=-\delta(\cos\theta,\sin\theta)
\]

with \(\alpha,\beta,\gamma,\delta>0\).  
Since the diagonals are equal,

\[
|WY|=\alpha+\beta=|XZ|=\gamma+\delta\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad 
\delta=\alpha+\beta-\gamma .
\]

---

### Point \(N\)

\(N\) is the intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of \(WX\) and \(YZ\).

The perpendicular bisector of a segment with endpoints \(A,B\) is the line through \(\frac{A+B}{2}\) whose direction vector is obtained by rotating \(B-A\) by \(90^{\circ}\).

Carrying this out for \(WX\) and \(YZ\) and solving for the intersection gives

\[
N=\Bigl(\frac{\alpha-\gamma}{2},\;
-\frac{\alpha-\gamma}{2}\,\frac{1+\cos\theta}{\sin\theta}\Bigr)
      =\frac{\alpha-\gamma}{2}\Bigl(1,\;-\frac{1+\cos\theta}{\sin\theta}\Bigr).
\]

Thus the vector \(\overrightarrow{MN}\) is proportional to  

\[
\mathbf{n}=\Bigl(1,\;-\frac{1+\cos\theta}{\sin\theta}\Bigr).
\]

---

### Point \(O\)

Similarly, \(O\) is the intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of \(XY\) and \(WZ\).  
A similar computation yields

\[
O=\Bigl(\frac{\gamma-\beta}{2},\;
\frac{\gamma-\beta}{2}\,\frac{1-\cos\theta}{\sin\theta}\Bigr)
      =\frac{\gamma-\beta}{2}\Bigl(1,\;\frac{1-\cos\theta}{\sin\theta}\Bigr),
\]

so  

\[
\mathbf{o}= \Bigl(1,\;\frac{1-\cos\theta}{\sin\theta}\Bigr)
\]

is a direction vector of \(\overrightarrow{MO}\).

---

### Angle \(\angle NMO\)

Compute the dot product of the two direction vectors:

\[
\mathbf{n}\cdot\mathbf{o}=1-\frac{1+\cos\theta}{\sin\theta}\,
                         \frac{1-\cos\theta}{\sin\theta}
                     =1-\frac{1-\cos^{2}\theta}{\sin^{2}\theta}
                     =1-1=0 .
\]

Hence \(\mathbf{n}\perp\mathbf{o}\); the lines \(MN\) and \(MO\) are perpendicular, so

\[
\angle NMO = 90^{\circ}.
\]

Therefore  

\[
\frac{\angle NMO}{5}= \frac{90^{\circ}}{5}=18^{\circ}.
\]

\[
\boxed{18^{\circ}}
\]

