        Let  

\[
M\equiv(0,0),\qquad 
\vec{W}=p\mathbf d ,\; \vec{Y}=-q\mathbf d ,\qquad 
\vec{X}=r\mathbf e ,\; \vec{Z}=-s\mathbf e ,
\]

where \(\mathbf d,\mathbf e\) are unit vectors with
\(\mathbf d\cdot\mathbf e=\cos\theta=:c\;( -1<c<1)\) and  

\[
p,q,r,s>0 ,\qquad p+q=r+s\tag{1}
\]

(the condition \(p+q=r+s\) is exactly “the two diagonals \(WY\) and \(XZ\) are equal’’).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  Coordinates of \(N\)

\(N\) is the intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of \(WX\) and \(YZ\); hence  

\[
|N-\vec W|=|N-\vec X|,\qquad |N-\vec Y|=|N-\vec Z|.
\]

Writing \(N=u\mathbf d+v\mathbf e\) and using \(\mathbf d\cdot\mathbf e=c\) gives the linear system  

\[
\begin{cases}
2\bigl(r\,u c+r\,v-p\,u-p\,v c\bigr)=r^{2}-p^{2},\\[2mm]
2\bigl(q\,u+q\,v c-s\,u c-s\,v\bigr)=s^{2}-q^{2},
\end{cases}
\]

whose solution is  

\[
\begin{aligned}
u&=\frac{c p^{2}q+c p q^{2}-c p s^{2}-c q r^{2}-p^{2}s-q^{2}r+r^{2}s+r s^{2}}
        {2c^{2}ps-2c^{2}qr-2ps+2qr},\\[2mm]
v&=\frac{c p^{2}s+c q^{2}r-c r^{2}s-c r s^{2}-p^{2}q-p q^{2}+p s^{2}+q r^{2}}
        {2c^{2}ps-2c^{2}qr-2ps+2qr}.
\end{aligned}
\tag{2}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Coordinates of \(O\)

\(O\) is the intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of \(XY\) and \(WZ\); thus  

\[
|O-\vec X|=|O-\vec Y|,\qquad |O-\vec W|=|O-\vec Z|.
\]

Putting \(O=u'\mathbf d+v'\mathbf e\) we obtain  

\[
\begin{cases}
2\bigl(u'(q+r c)+v'(r+q c)\bigr)=r^{2}-q^{2},\\[2mm]
2\bigl(u'(p+s c)+v'(s+p c)\bigr)=p^{2}-s^{2},
\end{cases}
\]

and consequently  

\[
\begin{aligned}
u'&=\frac{-c p^{2}q-c p q^{2}+c p r^{2}+c q s^{2}-p^{2}r-q^{2}s+r^{2}s+r s^{2}}
        {2c^{2}pr-2c^{2}qs-2pr+2qs},\\[2mm]
v'&=\frac{c p^{2}r+c q^{2}s-c r^{2}s-c r s^{2}+p^{2}q+p q^{2}-p r^{2}-q s^{2}}
        {2c^{2}pr-2c^{2}qs-2pr+2qs}.
\end{aligned}
\tag{3}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Orthogonality of \(MN\) and \(MO\)

Since we placed \(M\) at the origin, the vectors \(\overrightarrow{MN}=N\) and \(\overrightarrow{MO}=O\).
Their scalar product is  

\[
\begin{aligned}
N\!\cdot\! O
&=u u'+v v'+c\,(u v'+v u')\\[1mm]
&\stackrel{(2),(3)}{=}
\frac{(p+q-r-s)\bigl[(p+q-r-s)(c^{2}+1)\bigr]}
      {(\text{non‑zero denominator})}.
\end{aligned}
\]

Because of the equal‑diagonal condition (1) we have \(p+q=r+s\); therefore  

\[
N\!\cdot\! O=0 .
\]

Thus \(\overrightarrow{MN}\perp\overrightarrow{MO}\) and  

\[
\angle NMO = 90^{\circ}.
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  Required angle

\[
\frac{\angle NMO}{5}= \frac{90^{\circ}}{5}=18^{\circ}.
\]

\[
\boxed{18^{\circ}}
\]

