        [Turn 6 REASONING] (2481 chars)
        Let the intersection of the diagonals be the origin \(M=(0,0)\).
Put the vertices on the two diagonals:

\[
W=(-a,0),\qquad Y=(a,0),\qquad 
X=(0,b),\qquad Z=(0,-c),
\]

so that the diagonal \(WY\) is the \(x\)-axis and the diagonal \(XZ\) is the
\(y\)-axis.  
Since the diagonals are equal,

\[
|WY|=|XZ|\Longrightarrow 2a=b+c\qquad\text{(1)} .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### The point \(N\)

\(N\) is the intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of \(WX\) and \(YZ\).

* Mid‑point of \(WX\): \(\displaystyle M_{WX}= \Bigl(-\frac a2,\frac b2\Bigr)\).
  The direction vector of \(WX\) is \((a,b)\); a perpendicular direction is
  \((-b,a)\).

* Mid‑point of \(YZ\): \(\displaystyle M_{YZ}= \Bigl(\frac a2,-\frac c2\Bigr)\).
  The direction vector of \(YZ\) is \((-a,-c)\); a perpendicular direction is
  \((c,-a)\).

Hence the two bisectors are  

\[
\begin{cases}
N = M_{WX}+t\,(-b,a),\\[2mm]
N = M_{YZ}+s\,(c,-a),
\end{cases}
\qquad (t,s\in\mathbb R).
\]

Solving for \(t,s\) gives \(t=s=-\dfrac12\); therefore  

\[
N =\Bigl(-\frac a2+\frac b2,\;-\frac a2+\frac b2\Bigr)
      =\frac{b-a}{2}\,(1,1). \tag{2}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### The point \(O\)

\(O\) is the intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of \(XY\) and \(WZ\).

* Mid‑point of \(XY\): \(\displaystyle M_{XY}= \Bigl(\frac a2,\frac b2\Bigr)\).
  Direction of \(XY\) is \((a,-b)\); a perpendicular direction is \((b,a)\).

* Mid‑point of \(WZ\): \(\displaystyle M_{WZ}= \Bigl(-\frac a2,-\frac c2\Bigr)\).
  Direction of \(WZ\) is \((a,-c)\); a perpendicular direction is \((c,a)\).

Thus  

\[
\begin{cases}
O = M_{XY}+u\,(b,a),\\[2mm]
O = M_{WZ}+v\,(c,a),
\end{cases}
\qquad (u,v\in\mathbb R),
\]

and solving yields \(u=-\dfrac12,\;v=\dfrac12\). Consequently  

\[
O =\Bigl(\frac a2-\frac b2,\;-\frac a2+\frac b2\Bigr)
      =\frac{a-b}{2}\,(1,-1). \tag{3}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### Angle \(\angle NMO\)

From (2) and (3),

\[
\vec{MN}= \frac{b-a}{2}(1,1),\qquad
\vec{MO}= \frac{a-b}{2}(1,-1).
\]

Their dot product is

\[
\vec{MN}\cdot\vec{MO}
   =\frac{b-a}{2}\frac{a-b}{2}\bigl(1\cdot1+1\cdot(-1)\bigr)=0 .
\]

Hence \(\vec{MN}\perp\vec{MO}\) and  

\[
\angle NMO = 90^{\circ}.
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### Required value

\[
\frac{\angle NMO}{5}= \frac{90^{\circ}}{5}=18^{\circ}.
\]

\[
\boxed{18^{\circ}}
\]

