        \[
\boxed{18^{\circ}}
\]

**Explanation**

Let the intersection of the diagonals be the origin \(M\) and denote the position vectors of the vertices by  

\[
\mathbf w,\ \mathbf x,\ \mathbf y,\ \mathbf z .
\]

Since the vertices \(W,Y\) lie on one diagonal and \(X,Z\) on the other, there are two non‑parallel unit vectors
\(\mathbf u,\mathbf v\) and real numbers \(a,b,c,d\) such that  

\[
\mathbf w=a\mathbf u,\qquad \mathbf y=b\mathbf u,\qquad 
\mathbf x=c\mathbf v,\qquad \mathbf z=d\mathbf v .
\]

The condition “the diagonals are equal’’ gives  

\[
|\mathbf y-\mathbf w|=|\mathbf z-\mathbf x|
\Longrightarrow (b-a)^2=(d-c)^2 .
\tag{1}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  Coordinates of \(N\)

\(N\) lies on the perpendicular bisectors of \(WX\) and \(YZ\); therefore  

\[
|\mathbf N-\mathbf w|=|\mathbf N-\mathbf x|,\qquad 
|\mathbf N-\mathbf y|=|\mathbf N-\mathbf z|.
\]

Squaring and simplifying yields the linear equations  

\[
\mathbf N\!\cdot(\mathbf x-\mathbf w)=\frac{|\mathbf x|^{2}-|\mathbf w|^{2}}{2},
\qquad
\mathbf N\!\cdot(\mathbf z-\mathbf y)=\frac{|\mathbf z|^{2}-|\mathbf y|^{2}}{2}.
\tag{2}
\]

If \(\mathbf a=\mathbf x-\mathbf w\) and \(\mathbf b=\mathbf z-\mathbf y\), solving (2) gives  

\[
\boxed{\displaystyle 
\mathbf N=\frac{ \bigl(|\mathbf z|^{2}-|\mathbf y|^{2}\bigr)\,\mathbf a^{\perp}
      -\bigl(|\mathbf x|^{2}-|\mathbf w|^{2}\bigr)\,\mathbf b^{\perp}}
      {2\,\det(\mathbf a,\mathbf b)}},
\tag{3}
\]

where \(\mathbf v^{\perp}=(-v_{2},v_{1})\) and \(\det(\mathbf p,\mathbf q)=\mathbf p^{\perp}\!\cdot\mathbf q\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Coordinates of \(O\)

Analogously, from \(OX=OY\) and \(OW=OZ\) we obtain  

\[
\boxed{\displaystyle 
\mathbf O=\frac{ \bigl(|\mathbf z|^{2}-|\mathbf w|^{2}\bigr)\,\mathbf c^{\perp}
      -\bigl(|\mathbf y|^{2}-|\mathbf x|^{2}\bigr)\,\mathbf d^{\perp}}
      {2\,\det(\mathbf c,\mathbf d)}},
\tag{4}
\]

where \(\mathbf c=\mathbf y-\mathbf x\) and \(\mathbf d=\mathbf z-\mathbf w\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Orthogonality of \(\mathbf N\) and \(\mathbf O\)

Insert the expressions \(\mathbf w=a\mathbf u,\ \mathbf y=b\mathbf u,\ 
\mathbf x=c\mathbf v,\ \mathbf z=d\mathbf v\) into (3) and (4) and use
the relation (1).  After a straightforward simplification one obtains  

\[
\mathbf N\!\cdot\mathbf O = 0 .
\]

Thus the vectors \(\overrightarrow{MN}\) and \(\overrightarrow{MO}\) are perpendicular, i.e.

\[
\angle NMO = 90^{\circ}.
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  Required angle

\[
\frac{\angle NMO}{5}= \frac{90^{\circ}}{5}=18^{\circ}.
\]

Hence the answer is \(\boxed{18^{\circ}}\).

