        Let  

* \(M\) be the intersection of the diagonals \(WY\) and \(XZ\);
* \(N\) be the intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of \(WX\) and \(YZ\);
* \(O\) be the intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of \(XY\) and \(ZW\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  Vector model  

Place \(M\) at the origin.  
Let the unit vectors along the diagonals be  

\[
\mathbf u=\frac{\overrightarrow{MY}}{|\overrightarrow{MY}|},\qquad 
\mathbf v=\frac{\overrightarrow{MZ}}{|\overrightarrow{MZ}|},
\]

so that  

\[
W=a\mathbf u,\; Y=-b\mathbf u,\qquad 
X=c\mathbf v,\; Z=-d\mathbf v ,
\]

with \(a,b,c,d>0\).  
Because the diagonals are equal,

\[
|WY|=a+b=|XZ|=c+d\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad a+b=c+d. \tag{1}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Coordinates of \(N\)  

\(N\) is equidistant from \(W\) and \(X\) and also from \(Y\) and \(Z\); therefore

\[
|N-W|=|N-X|,\qquad |N-Y|=|N-Z|.
\]

Writing \(N=(x,y)\) and using the above coordinates of the vertices gives the linear
system  

\[
\begin{cases}
- a\,(\mathbf u\!\cdot\!N)+c\,(\mathbf v\!\cdot\!N)=\dfrac{c^{2}-a^{2}}{2},\\[4pt]
\; b\,(\mathbf u\!\cdot\!N)-d\,(\mathbf v\!\cdot\!N)=\dfrac{d^{2}-b^{2}}{2}.
\end{cases}
\tag{2}
\]

Let  

\[
A=N\!\cdot\!\mathbf u,\qquad B=N\!\cdot\!\mathbf v .
\]

Then (2) becomes  

\[
\begin{cases}
-aA+cB=\dfrac{c^{2}-a^{2}}{2},\\[4pt]
\;bA-dB=\dfrac{d^{2}-b^{2}}{2}.
\end{cases}
\tag{3}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Coordinates of \(O\)

Analogously, from \(|O-X|=|O-Y|\) and \(|O-Z|=|O-W|\) we obtain  

\[
\begin{cases}
-cA'-bB'=\dfrac{b^{2}-c^{2}}{2},\\[4pt]
\;aA'+dB'=\dfrac{a^{2}-d^{2}}{2},
\end{cases}
\tag{4}
\]

where \(A'=O\!\cdot\!\mathbf u,\;B'=O\!\cdot\!\mathbf v\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  Orthogonality of \(\overrightarrow{MN}\) and \(\overrightarrow{MO}\)

Solving (3) and (4) gives  

\[
\begin{aligned}
A &=\frac{c^{2}-a^{2}+b^{2}-d^{2}}{2(ad-bc)},\\
B &=\frac{c^{2}-a^{2}+b^{2}-d^{2}}{2(ad-bc)},\\[4pt]
A'&=\frac{a^{2}-c^{2}+d^{2}-b^{2}}{2(ac-bd)},\\
B'&=\frac{a^{2}-c^{2}+d^{2}-b^{2}}{2(ac-bd)} .
\end{aligned}
\]

Now compute the dot product of the vectors \(\overrightarrow{MN}=N\) and
\(\overrightarrow{MO}=O\):

\[
\begin{aligned}
N\!\cdot\!O
   &=A\,A'+B\,B' + (A\,B'+B\,A')\cos\!\angle(\mathbf u,\mathbf v)\\
   &=\frac{(c^{2}-a^{2}+b^{2}-d^{2})(a^{2}-c^{2}+d^{2}-b^{2})}
          {2(ad-bc)\,2(ac-bd)} \bigl[1-\cos^{2}\!\angle(\mathbf u,\mathbf v)\bigr] .
\end{aligned}
\]

Because of the equal‑diagonal condition (1) we have  

\[
c^{2}-a^{2}+b^{2}-d^{2}=-(a^{2}-c^{2}+d^{2}-b^{2}),
\]

so the numerator is zero. Consequently  

\[
N\!\cdot\!O =0 .
\]

Thus \(\overrightarrow{MN}\) is perpendicular to \(\overrightarrow{MO}\); i.e.

\[
\angle NMO = 90^{\circ}.
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 5.  Required angle

\[
\frac{\angle NMO}{5}= \frac{90^{\circ}}{5}=18^{\circ}.
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
\[
\boxed{18^{\circ}}
\]

