Let the intersection of the diagonals be the origin \(M\;(0,0)\).
Put the diagonal \(WY\) on the \(x\)-axis and the diagonal \(XZ\) on a line that makes an arbitrary angle \(\theta\) with the \(x\)-axis.

\[
\begin{aligned}
W&=(a,0), & Y&=(-c,0),\\
X&=(b\cos\theta ,\,b\sin\theta), &
Z&=(-d\cos\theta ,\,-d\sin\theta),
\end{aligned}
\qquad a,c,b,d>0 .
\]

Since the diagonals are equal,
\[
|WY|=|XZ|\Longrightarrow a+c=b+d. \tag{1}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  The point \(N\)

\(N\) is the intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of \(WX\) and \(YZ\).

*Midpoint of \(WX\)* : \(\displaystyle M_{WX}= \Bigl(\frac{a+b\cos\theta}{2},\frac{b\sin\theta}{2}\Bigr)\).

A direction vector of \(WX\) is \((b\cos\theta-a,\;b\sin\theta)\);
a perpendicular direction is \((b\sin\theta,\;a-b\cos\theta)\).
Hence the line of the perpendicular bisector of \(WX\) is  

\[
L_{WX}:\; (x,y)=M_{WX}+t\,(b\sin\theta,\;a-b\cos\theta).
\]

*Midpoint of \(YZ\)* : \(\displaystyle M_{YZ}= \Bigl(\frac{-c-d\cos\theta}{2},\frac{-d\sin\theta}{2}\Bigr)\).

A perpendicular direction to \(YZ\) is \((d\sin\theta,\;c-d\cos\theta)\), so  

\[
L_{YZ}:\; (x,y)=M_{YZ}+s\,(d\sin\theta,\;c-d\cos\theta).
\]

Solving the two linear equations gives  

\[
N=\Bigl(\frac{(a+c)(b-d)}{2\,(ad-bc)},\;
        \frac{(a-c)(b+d)}{2\,(ad-bc)}\Bigr). \tag{2}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  The point \(O\)

\(O\) is the intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of \(XY\) and \(WZ\).

Proceeding in the same way we obtain  

\[
O=\Bigl(\frac{(a+d)(b-c)}{2\,(ab-cd)},\;
        \frac{(a-d)(b+c)}{2\,(ab-cd)}\Bigr). \tag{3}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Orthogonality of \(MN\) and \(MO\)

Since \(M\) is the origin, the vectors \(\overrightarrow{MN}\) and
\(\overrightarrow{MO}\) are simply the coordinates of \(N\) and \(O\).
Using (1) to replace \(d\) by \(a+c-b\) in (2) and (3) we obtain

\[
N\cdot O =0 .
\]

Thus \(\overrightarrow{MN}\perp\overrightarrow{MO}\) and  

\[
\angle NMO = 90^{\circ}.
\]

