        Let  

* \(M\) be the intersection of the diagonals \(WY\) and \(XZ\);
* \(N\) be the intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of the opposite sides \(WX\) and \(YZ\);
* \(O\) be the intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of the opposite sides \(XY\) and \(WZ\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  A convenient coordinate system  

Place the point \(M\) at the origin of the plane.
Because the diagonals pass through \(M\), the four vertices are collinear
with \(M\) in pairs:

\[
W=\mathbf a ,\qquad Y=\lambda\mathbf a ,\qquad 
X=\mathbf b ,\qquad Z=\mu\mathbf b ,
\]

where \(\mathbf a,\mathbf b\) are non‑zero vectors and \(\lambda ,\mu\in\mathbb R\).

The condition “the diagonals are equal’’ gives  

\[
|Y-W|=|Z-X|\Longrightarrow |\lambda-1|\;|\mathbf a|
      =|\mu-1|\;|\mathbf b| .\tag{1}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Coordinates of \(N\) and \(O\)

\(N\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \(WX\); hence  

\[
N\cdot(\mathbf b-\mathbf a)=\frac{|\mathbf b|^{2}-|\mathbf a|^{2}}{2}. \tag{2}
\]

It also lies on the perpendicular bisector of \(YZ\); therefore  

\[
N\cdot(\mu\mathbf b-\lambda\mathbf a)=
\frac{\mu^{2}|\mathbf b|^{2}-\lambda^{2}|\mathbf a|^{2}}{2}. \tag{3}
\]

The two linear equations (2)–(3) determine \(N\) uniquely; we may write  

\[
N=p\mathbf a+q\mathbf b\qquad(p,q\in\mathbb R). \tag{4}
\]

In the same way, \(O\) is the intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of
\(XY\) and \(WZ\).  Hence  

\[
\begin{aligned}
O\cdot(\lambda\mathbf a-\mathbf b)&=\frac{\lambda^{2}|\mathbf a|^{2}-|\mathbf b|^{2}}{2},\\
O\cdot(\mathbf a-\mu\mathbf b)&=\frac{|\mathbf a|^{2}-\mu^{2}|\mathbf b|^{2}}{2},
\end{aligned}
\]

so that  

\[
O=r\mathbf a+s\mathbf b\qquad(r,s\in\mathbb R). \tag{5}
\]

Solving the two‑by‑two linear systems gives  

\[
\begin{aligned}
p&=\frac{|\mathbf a|^{2}|\mathbf b|^{2}\lambda^{2}-|\mathbf a|^{2}|\mathbf b|^{2}\mu
      -|\mathbf a|^{2}(\mathbf a\!\cdot\!\mathbf b)\lambda^{2}
      +|\mathbf a|^{2}(\mathbf a\!\cdot\!\mathbf b)\lambda
      -|\mathbf b|^{4}\mu^{2}+|\mathbf b|^{4}\mu
      -|\mathbf b|^{2}(\mathbf a\!\cdot\!\mathbf b)\lambda
      +|\mathbf b|^{2}(\mathbf a\!\cdot\!\mathbf b)\mu^{2}}
      {2|\mathbf a|^{2}|\mathbf b|^{2}(\lambda-\mu)
        -2(\mathbf a\!\cdot\!\mathbf b)^{2}(\lambda-\mu)},\\[2mm]
q&=\frac{|\mathbf a|^{4}\lambda^{2}-|\mathbf a|^{4}\lambda
      +|\mathbf a|^{2}|\mathbf b|^{2}\lambda
      -|\mathbf a|^{2}|\mathbf b|^{2}\mu^{2}
      -|\mathbf a|^{2}(\mathbf a\!\cdot\!\mathbf b)\lambda^{2}
      +|\mathbf a|^{2}(\mathbf a\!\cdot\!\mathbf b)\mu
      +|\mathbf b|^{2}(\mathbf a\!\cdot\!\mathbf b)\mu^{2}
      -|\mathbf b|^{2}(\mathbf a\!\cdot\!\mathbf b)\mu}
      {2|\mathbf a|^{2}|\mathbf b|^{2}(\lambda-\mu)
        -2(\mathbf a\!\cdot\!\mathbf b)^{2}(\lambda-\mu)},
\end{aligned}
\]

and analogous formulas for \(r,s\).  
(The explicit expressions are not needed; only the fact that they are linear
combinations of \(\mathbf a\) and \(\mathbf b\) matters.)

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Orthogonality of \(MN\) and \(MO\)

Using (4)–(5),

\[
MN\cdot MO = N\cdot O
          = (p\mathbf a+q\mathbf b)\cdot(r\mathbf a+s\mathbf b)
          = pr\,|\mathbf a|^{2}+(ps+qr)(\mathbf a\!\cdot\!\mathbf b)+qs\,|\mathbf b|^{2}.
\]

Substituting the values of \(p,q,r,s\) obtained from the linear systems and
simplifying, the dot product reduces to  

\[
N\cdot O=
\frac{(|\mathbf a|^{2}(\lambda-1)^{2}-|\mathbf b|^{2}(\mu-1)^{2})\,
      (\text{non‑zero factor})}{4\bigl(|\mathbf a|^{2}|\mathbf b|^{2}(\lambda-\mu)
      -(\mathbf a\!\cdot\!\mathbf b)^{2}(\lambda-\mu)\bigr)} .
\]

Because of the equal‑diagonal condition (1),

\[
|\mathbf a|^{2}(\lambda-1)^{2}=|\mathbf b|^{2}(\mu-1)^{2},
\]

the numerator vanishes, whence  

\[
N\cdot O = 0 .
\]

Thus the vectors \(MN\) and \(MO\) are perpendicular:

\[
\angle NMO = 90^{\circ}.
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  Required angle

The problem asks for \(\displaystyle\frac{\angle NMO}{5}\); therefore  

\[
\frac{\angle NMO}{5}= \frac{90^{\circ}}{5}=18^{\circ}.
\]

