        [Turn 13 REASONING] (2429 chars)
        Let  

\[
M=W\!X\cap YZ ,\qquad 
N= \text{perp. bisector of }WX\;\cap\; \text{perp. bisector of }YZ,
\qquad 
O= \text{perp. bisector of }XY\;\cap\; \text{perp. bisector of }WZ .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  A convenient vector description  

Put the intersection point \(M\) at the origin.  
Since the two diagonals meet at \(M\),

\[
W,\;Y\ \text{are collinear with }M,\qquad 
X,\;Z\ \text{are collinear with }M .
\]

Hence there exist two unit vectors \(u,\;v\) (the directions of the
diagonals) and real numbers \(p,q,r,s\) such that  

\[
W=p\,u,\qquad Y=q\,u,\qquad X=r\,v,\qquad Z=s\,v .
\]

The condition “the diagonals are equal’’ gives  

\[
|WY|=|XZ|\Longrightarrow |q-p|=|s-r|.
\tag{1}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Coordinates of \(N\) and \(O\)

The perpendicular bisector of a segment \(AB\) is characterised by  

\[
\bigl(P-\tfrac{A+B}{2}\bigr)\cdot (B-A)=0 .
\]

Applying this to the two pairs of opposite sides we obtain the
equations for \(N\):

\[
\begin{cases}
\bigl(N-\tfrac{p\,u+r\,v}{2}\bigr)\!\cdot\!(r\,v-p\,u)=0,\\[2mm]
\bigl(N-\tfrac{q\,u+s\,v}{2}\bigr)\!\cdot\!(s\,v-q\,u)=0 .
\end{cases}
\]

Writing \(N=a\,u+b\,v\) and solving the linear system yields  

\[
\boxed{\,N=
\frac{(q-p)(r-s)}{2\bigl((q-p)^2-(r-s)^2\bigr)}\;
\bigl((q-p)u-(r-s)v\bigr)} .
\tag{2}
\]

In the same way, from the bisectors of \(XY\) and \(WZ\) we get, with
\(O=c\,u+d\,v\),

\[
\boxed{\,O=
\frac{(q-p)(r-s)}{2\bigl((q-p)^2-(r-s)^2\bigr)}\;
\bigl((q-p)u+(r-s)v\bigr)} .
\tag{3}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Orthogonality of \(MN\) and \(MO\)

Because we placed \(M\) at the origin, \(MN=N\) and \(MO=O\).
Using (2) and (3),

\[
\begin{aligned}
MN\cdot MO
&=N\cdot O \\
&=\frac{(q-p)^2(r-s)^2}{4\bigl((q-p)^2-(r-s)^2\bigr)^2}
   \Bigl[(u\cdot u)-(v\cdot v)\Bigr] .
\end{aligned}
\]

Since \(u\) and \(v\) are unit vectors,
\(u\cdot u=v\cdot v=1\); therefore the bracket equals \(0\) and

\[
MN\cdot MO =0 .
\]

Thus \(MN\perp MO\) and  

\[
\boxed{\;\angle NMO = 90^{\circ}\; } .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  Required angle

The problem asks for \(\displaystyle\frac{\angle NMO}{5}\):

\[
\frac{90^{\circ}}{5}=18^{\circ}.
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
\[
\boxed{18^{\circ}}
\]

