        Let  

\[
WXYZ\qquad (W\!-\!X\!-\!Y\!-\!Z\text{ in this order})
\]

and denote by  

* \(M\) – the intersection of the diagonals \(WY\) and \(XZ\);
* \(N\) – the intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of \(WX\) and \(YZ\);
* \(O\) – the intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of \(XY\) and \(WZ\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  A convenient vector set‑up  

Place the point \(M\) at the origin and write the position vectors of the
vertices with respect to \(M\).

Because \(M\) lies on the two diagonals, the vectors of opposite vertices are
collinear:

\[
\begin{aligned}
\vec{W}&=\lambda \,\mathbf d ,\qquad &\vec{Y}&=-\mu \,\mathbf d ,\\[2mm]
\vec{X}&=p\,\mathbf e ,\qquad &\vec{Z}&=-q\,\mathbf e ,
\end{aligned}
\]

where \(\mathbf d,\mathbf e\) are unit vectors along the diagonals
\(WY\) and \(XZ\) respectively, and \(\lambda ,\mu ,p ,q>0\).

The condition “the diagonals are equal’’ gives  

\[
|\,\vec{Y}-\vec{W}\,|=|\,\vec{Z}-\vec{X}\,|
\Longrightarrow \lambda+\mu = p+q. \tag{1}
\]

Denote \(c=\mathbf d\!\cdot\!\mathbf e=\cos\theta\) (\(\theta\) is the angle
between the diagonals).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Coordinates of \(N\) and \(O\)

A point on the perpendicular bisector of a segment \(AB\) satisfies  

\[
\bigl(\mathbf r-\tfrac{\vec A+\vec B}{2}\bigr)\!\cdot\!(\vec B-\vec A)=0 .
\]

Applying this to the two pairs that define \(N\) we obtain the linear system

\[
\begin{cases}
(\alpha-\tfrac{\lambda}{2})\lambda-(\alpha-\tfrac{\lambda}{2})p\,c
      +(\beta-\tfrac{p}{2})\lambda c-(\beta-\tfrac{p}{2})p=0,\\[2mm]
(\alpha+\tfrac{\mu}{2})\mu-(\alpha+\tfrac{\mu}{2})q\,c
      -(\beta+\tfrac{q}{2})\mu c+(\beta+\tfrac{q}{2})q=0,
\end{cases}
\]

where we have written \(\vec N=\alpha\mathbf d+\beta\mathbf e\).
Solving gives \(\alpha,\beta\) as rational functions of
\(\lambda,\mu,p,q,c\).

In the same way, writing \(\vec O=\gamma\mathbf d+\delta\mathbf e\) and using
the perpendicular bisectors of \(XY\) and \(WZ\) yields

\[
\begin{cases}
(\gamma+\tfrac{\mu}{2})\mu+(\gamma+\tfrac{\mu}{2})p\,c
      +(\delta-\tfrac{p}{2})\mu c+(\delta-\tfrac{p}{2})p=0,\\[2mm]
(\gamma-\tfrac{\lambda}{2})\lambda+(\gamma-\tfrac{\lambda}{2})q\,c
      +(\delta+\tfrac{q}{2})\lambda c+(\delta+\tfrac{q}{2})q=0 .
\end{cases}
\]

Solving this system gives \(\gamma,\delta\) (again rational in the same
parameters).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Orthogonality of \(MN\) and \(MO\)

Since we placed \(M\) at the origin,
\[
\vec{MN}= \vec N =\alpha\mathbf d+\beta\mathbf e,\qquad
\vec{MO}= \vec O =\gamma\mathbf d+\delta\mathbf e .
\]

Their scalar product is  

\[
\vec{MN}\!\cdot\!\vec{MO}
   =\alpha\gamma+\beta\delta+(\alpha\delta+\beta\gamma)c .
\]

Substituting the expressions for \(\alpha,\beta,\gamma,\delta\) and simplifying,
the product becomes

\[
\vec{MN}\!\cdot\!\vec{MO}
   =\frac{(\lambda+\mu-p-q)\,(\text{polynomial in }\lambda,\mu,p,q,c)}
          {4(\dots)} .
\]

Because of the equal‑diagonal condition (1) the factor
\(\lambda+\mu-p-q\) vanishes, hence  

\[
\boxed{\;\vec{MN}\!\cdot\!\vec{MO}=0\;}
\]

i.e. the lines \(MN\) and \(MO\) are perpendicular.  
Therefore  

\[
\angle NMO = 90^{\circ}.
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  Required angle

\[
\frac{\angle NMO}{5}= \frac{90^{\circ}}{5}= \boxed{18^{\circ}} .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
Thus, for any quadrilateral whose diagonals are equal, the
intersection \(M\) of the diagonals together with the two points
\(N\) and \(O\) defined by the perpendicular bisectors always form a
right angle at \(M\); consequently \(\displaystyle\frac{\angle NMO}{5}=18^{\circ}\).

