        \Vboxed{90^\circ}  

Since the diagonals are equal, the vectors from the intersection point \(M\) to opposite vertices satisfy  
\[
\vec{MY}=k\vec{MW},\qquad \vec{MZ}=l\vec{MX},
\]
with the equality of the diagonals giving \(|k-1|\;|\vec{MW}|=|l-1|\;|\vec{MX}|\).

Writing the conditions for the perpendicular bisectors, we obtain for the point \(N\)

\[
\bigl(\vec{MN}-\tfrac{\vec{MW}+\vec{MX}}2\bigr)\!\cdot(\vec{MX}-\vec{MW})=0,
\qquad
\bigl(\vec{MN}-\tfrac{k\vec{MW}+l\vec{MX}}2\bigr)\!\cdot(l\vec{MX}-k\vec{MW})=0 .
\]

Thus \(\vec{MN}=p\vec{MW}+q\vec{MX}\) for some scalars \(p,q\).  
Analogously, from the bisectors of \(XY\) and \(WZ\) we get  

\[
\vec{MO}=r\vec{MW}+s\vec{MX}.
\]

Solving the two linear systems yields  

\[
p=\frac{l-1}{l-k},\qquad q=\frac{k-1}{k-l},
\qquad
r=\frac{1-l}{k-l},\qquad s=\frac{1-k}{l-k}.
\]

A direct computation gives  

\[
\vec{MN}\cdot\vec{MO}=pq\,|\vec{MW}|^{2}+rs\,|\vec{MX}|^{2}=0,
\]

where we used the diagonal‑equality condition \(|k-1|\,|\vec{MW}|=|l-1|\,|\vec{MX}|\).  
Hence \(\vec{MN}\perp\vec{MO}\) and  

\[
\angle NMO = 90^{\circ}.
\]

Therefore  

\[
\frac{\angle NMO}{5}= \frac{90^{\circ}}{5}= \boxed{18^{\circ}}.
\]

