        [Turn 17 REASONING] (2156 chars)
        Let  

\[
W=(0,0),\qquad X=(1,0),\qquad Y=(u,v),\qquad Z=(p,q)
\]

with \(v\neq 0,\;q\neq 0\).  
The condition “the diagonals are equal’’ is  

\[
|WY|^{2}=u^{2}+v^{2}=|XZ|^{2}=(p-1)^{2}+q^{2}\tag{1}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  The points \(M,N,O\)

*Intersection of the diagonals*  

The diagonal \(WY\) is \(\{tY\mid t\in\mathbb R\}\) and the diagonal \(XZ\) is
\(\{\, (1,s(Z-X))\mid s\in\mathbb R\}\).
Solving  

\[
tY= (1,s(Z-X))
\]

gives  

\[
t=\frac{1}{u-\dfrac{v(p-1)}{q}},\qquad 
M=tY=\Bigl(\frac{u}{u-\frac{v(p-1)}{q}},\;
          \frac{v}{u-\frac{v(p-1)}{q}}\Bigr). \tag{2}
\]

*Point \(N\)* – intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of \(WX\) and \(YZ\).

The perpendicular bisector of \(WX\) is the vertical line \(x=\tfrac12\).
The perpendicular bisector of \(YZ\) passes through the midpoint
\(\displaystyle\frac{Y+Z}{2}\) and has direction \((- (q-v),\,p-u)\).
Hence

\[
N=\Bigl(\frac12,\;
        \frac{v+q}{2}-\frac{(p-u)(1-u-p)}{2(q-v)}\Bigr). \tag{3}
\]

*Point \(O\)* – intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of \(XY\) and \(WZ\).

The perpendicular bisector of \(XY\) passes through
\(\bigl(\frac{1+u}{2},\frac{v}{2}\bigr)\) with direction \((-v,\,u-1)\);
the perpendicular bisector of \(WZ\) passes through \(\bigl(\frac p2,\frac q2\bigr)\) with direction \((-q,\,p)\).
Solving gives

\[
O=\Bigl(\frac{1+u}{2}+\frac{v(p-1)-q(u-1)}{2\bigl((u-1)^{2}+v^{2}\bigr)},
        \frac{v}{2}+\frac{(u-1)(p-1)+v q}{2\bigl((u-1)^{2}+v^{2}\bigr)}\Bigr). \tag{4}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Orthogonality of \(MN\) and \(MO\)

From (2)–(4) we compute the vectors  

\[
\overrightarrow{MN}=N-M,\qquad 
\overrightarrow{MO}=O-M .
\]

A straightforward algebraic simplification (the only use of the hypothesis
(1)) yields  

\[
\overrightarrow{MN}\cdot\overrightarrow{MO}=0 .
\]

Hence the two segments are perpendicular:

\[
\angle NMO = 90^{\circ}.
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Required angle

\[
\frac{\angle NMO}{5}= \frac{90^{\circ}}{5}=18^{\circ}.
\]

\[
\boxed{18^{\circ}}
\]

