--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  The cevian \(ZP\)

Let \(P\in XY\) and suppose  

\[
\angle PZY =2\angle ZXY =40^{\circ},\qquad 
\angle QPZ =2\angle PZY .
\]

Hence  

\[
\angle PZY =\angle ZXY =20^{\circ},\qquad 
\angle QPZ =40^{\circ}.
\]

Thus the ray \(ZP\) makes an angle of \(20^{\circ}\) with \(ZY\); consequently  

\[
\angle ZPY =100^{\circ},\qquad \angle XPZ =20^{\circ},\qquad 
\angle ZPX =80^{\circ}.
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Lengths on \(XY\)

Put \(XY=1\) and denote \(XP=a,\;PY=1-a\).

In \(\triangle ZPY\) we have the angles \(20^{\circ},60^{\circ},100^{\circ}\); by the law of sines  

\[
\frac{ZP}{\sin 60^{\circ}}=\frac{PY}{\sin 20^{\circ}} .
\tag{1}
\]

In the whole triangle \(XYZ\),

\[
\frac{ZY}{\sin 20^{\circ}}=\frac{XY}{\sin 100^{\circ}}= \frac{1}{\sin 80^{\circ}} .
\tag{2}
\]

From (1) and (2),

\[
ZY=\frac{\sin 100^{\circ}}{\sin 20^{\circ}}\,PY
      =\frac{\sin 20^{\circ}}{\sin 80^{\circ}} .
\]

Hence  

\[
PY=\Bigl(\frac{\sin 20^{\circ}}{\sin 80^{\circ}}\Bigr)^{\!2},
\qquad
XP=1-PY=\frac{\sin 60^{\circ}}{\sin 80^{\circ}} .
\tag{3}
\]

Thus \(XP= XZ\); the point \(P\) divides \(XY\) in the same ratio as the side
\(XZ\) of the original triangle.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  The point \(Q\) on \(XZ\)

Let \(Q\in XZ\) and denote \(ZQ\) by \(z\).
In \(\triangle QPZ\) the angles are  

\[
\angle ZQP =60^{\circ},\;
\angle QPZ =40^{\circ},\;
\angle QZP =80^{\circ}.
\]

Applying the law of sines,

\[
\frac{ZQ}{\sin 40^{\circ}}=\frac{ZP}{\sin 60^{\circ}}
      =\frac{PY}{\sin 20^{\circ}}\;\;(\text{by }(1)).
\]

Consequently  

\[
ZQ =\frac{\sin 40^{\circ}}{\sin 20^{\circ}}\,PY
    =\frac{\sin 40^{\circ}\,\sin 20^{\circ}}{\sin^{2}80^{\circ}} .
\tag{4}
\]

From (2) we have \(ZY=\dfrac{\sin 20^{\circ}}{\sin 80^{\circ}}\); therefore

\[
\frac{ZQ}{ZY}= \frac{\sin 40^{\circ}}{\sin 80^{\circ}} .
\tag{5}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  Triangle \(YQZ\)

In \(\triangle YQZ\) let  

\[
\angle YQZ =\beta ,\qquad
\angle QYZ =\gamma .
\]

We already know \(\angle Z =100^{\circ}\); hence  

\[
\beta+\gamma = 80^{\circ}. \tag{6}
\]

Using (5) and the law of sines in \(\triangle YQZ\),

\[
\frac{ZQ}{ZY}= \frac{\sin\gamma}{\sin\beta}
               =\frac{\sin 40^{\circ}}{\sin 80^{\circ}} .
\tag{7}
\]

From (6) write \(\gamma =80^{\circ}-\beta\) and substitute into (7):

\[
\frac{\sin(80^{\circ}-\beta)}{\sin\beta}
      =\frac{\sin 40^{\circ}}{\sin 80^{\circ}} .
\]

Using \(\sin(80^{\circ}-\beta)=\sin80^{\circ}\cos\beta-\cos80^{\circ}\sin\beta\) we obtain  

\[
\sin80^{\circ}\cot\beta-\cos80^{\circ}= \frac{\sin40^{\circ}}{\sin80^{\circ}} .
\]

Hence  

\[
\cot\beta =\frac{\cos80^{\circ}+\dfrac{\sin40^{\circ}}{\sin80^{\circ}}}{\sin80^{\circ}}
          =\frac{1}{\tan 50^{\circ}} .
\]

Thus  

\[
\beta =50^{\circ},\qquad \gamma =30^{\circ}.
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 5.  Desired angle \(\displaystyle\angle YQP\)

At point \(Q\) the three rays are \(QZ,\;QP,\;QY\).  
From \(\triangle QPZ\) we have  

\[
\angle ZQP =60^{\circ},
\]

and from \(\triangle YQZ\)

\[
\angle ZQY =\beta =50^{\circ}.
\]

Therefore  

\[
\angle YQP =\angle ZQP-\angle ZQY = 60^{\circ}-50^{\circ}=10^{\circ}.
\]

