--- [geometry.angle_chasing.directed_angles] ---
Triggers: cyclic quadrilateral, configuration cases, angles mod 180
Technique: Directed angles (mod 180°): ∠(AB,CD) is the signed angle from line AB to line CD, measured mod 180°. Key property: four points are concyclic iff ∠(PA,PB) = ∠(QA,QB) for any P,Q on the circle. Eliminates case analysis in angle chasing. Use for: proving concyclicity, tangency conditions, and collinearity via angle arguments.
Question: When a geometry problem requires proving concyclicity or angle equalities, and the configuration has multiple cases (acute vs obtuse). Directed angles mod 180° eliminate case analysis.
Answer: Directed angle rules: (1) ∠(AB,CD) = -∠(CD,AB) (antisymmetry). (2) ∠(AB,CD) = ∠(AB,EF) + ∠(EF,CD) (additivity). (3) Four points concyclic ⟺ ∠(AC,BC) = ∠(AD,BD). (4) AB tangent to circle through B,C,D ⟺ ∠(AB,BC) = ∠(BD,BC). When to use: any problem where you'd normally chase angles but worry about configuration cases (acute vs obtuse triangle, point inside vs outside circle). Directed angles automatically handle all cases. Common workflow: (a) Label all angles using directed notation. (b) Chain equalities: ∠(XA,XB) = ∠(YA,YB) iff X,Y,A,B concyclic. (c) Use transitivity to prove the target angle equality. Trap: directed angles work mod 180°, so they can't distinguish supplementary angles. Use unsigned angles when you need to distinguish 60° from 120°.

