        The directed graph formed by the “knows’’ relation has  

* 345 vertices,  
* out‑degree of every vertex = 9.

For a class assignment we need a proper colouring of the **underlying undirected graph** (an edge is present whenever at least one of the two directed arcs exists).  
Thus the required number of classes is the chromatic number \(\chi(G)\) of this undirected graph.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  An upper bound – the graph is 18‑degenerate  

Let \(H\subseteq G\) be any non‑empty set of vertices and let  

\[
e(H)=\text{number of undirected edges whose both ends lie in }H .
\]

Every vertex of \(H\) has at most 9 outgoing arcs, so the total number of
directed arcs that start in \(H\) is at most \(9|H|\).
Each undirected edge inside \(H\) contributes **at least one** of those arcs
(the tail of the arc is one of its two endpoints). Hence  

\[
e(H)\le 9|H|.
\]

Consequently the average degree inside \(H\) satisfies  

\[
\frac{2e(H)}{|H|}\le \frac{2\cdot 9|H|}{|H|}=18 .
\]

Thus every subgraph of \(G\) contains a vertex of degree at most \(18\).
Therefore the graph is **18‑degenerate**, and a greedy colouring in the
reverse order of removal uses at most  

\[
18+1 = 19
\]

colours. Hence  

\[
\boxed{\chi(G)\le 19}.
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  A lower bound – a configuration that needs 19 colours  

Take 19 students and arrange the “knows’’ relation as a **regular tournament**:
for every unordered pair \(\{u,v\}\) exactly one of the two directed arcs
\(u\to v\) or \(v\to u\) is present, and each vertex has out‑degree
\((19-1)/2 = 9\).  
Such a tournament exists because 19 is odd.

In this subgraph every pair of vertices is connected by at least one
directed arc, so the underlying undirected graph on these 19 vertices is
the complete graph \(K_{19}\).  Hence any proper colouring of the whole set of
students must colour these 19 vertices with 19 distinct colours:

\[
\boxed{\chi(G)\ge 19}.
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Minimal number of classes

The lower and upper bounds coincide, so the worst‑case chromatic number is
exactly 19.  Therefore, regardless of how the “knows’’ relation is arranged,
the students can always be partitioned into **19 classes**, and 19 classes are
sometimes necessary.

\[
\boxed{\,n_{\min}=19\,}
\]

