        [Turn 10 REASONING] (2133 chars)
        \[
x_k=\operatorname{lcm}(k,k+1,\dots ,k+n-1),\qquad n\in\mathbb Z_{>0}
\]

A sequence is *weakly increasing* if there is an integer \(N\) such that  

\[
x_{k+1}>x_k\qquad\text{for all }k>N .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  The cases \(n=1,2\)

* \(n=1\):  \(x_k=k\).  Obviously \(x_{k+1}=k+1>k=x_k\) for every \(k\).

* \(n=2\):  consecutive integers are coprime, hence  

\[
x_k=\operatorname{lcm}(k,k+1)=k(k+1).
\]

Then  

\[
x_{k+1}-x_k=(k+1)(k+2)-k(k+1)=2(k+1)>0,
\]

so the sequence is strictly increasing from the start.

Thus \(n=1,2\) satisfy the required property.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  No larger \(n\) works

Assume \(n\ge 3\).  
Choose any prime \(p>n\) (there are infinitely many).  
Set  

\[
k=p-n+1 .
\]

The first block of \(n\) consecutive integers is  

\[
S=\{p-n+1,\;p-n+2,\dots ,p\},
\]

and the next block is  

\[
S'=\{p-n+2,\;p-n+3,\dots ,p+1\}.
\]

*Because \(p\) is prime and \(p>n\), the only multiple of \(p\) among the
integers \(\le p+n-1\) is \(p\) itself.*  
Hence \(p\in S\) but \(p\notin S'\). Consequently  

\[
v_p\bigl(\operatorname{lcm}S\bigr)=1,\qquad 
v_p\bigl(\operatorname{lcm}S'\bigr)=0 .
\]

All other prime factors of \(\operatorname{lcm}S'\) are \(\le p-1\):
any new prime divisor can only come from the number \(p+1\), and
\(p+1\) is even, so every prime divisor of \(p+1\) is \(\le\frac{p+1}{2}<p\).

Therefore  

\[
\operatorname{lcm}S' = \frac{\operatorname{lcm}S}{p}\cdot M,
\qquad\text{with }M< p .
\]

Thus \(\operatorname{lcm}S' < \operatorname{lcm}S\); i.e.

\[
x_{k+1}= \operatorname{lcm}(k+1,\dots ,k+n) < 
\operatorname{lcm}(k,\dots ,k+n-1)=x_k .
\]

So for each prime \(p>n\) we obtain a decrease \(x_{k+1}<x_k\).
Since there are infinitely many such primes, the sequence \((x_k)\) has
infinitely many descents and can never become monotone after any index.

Hence **no \(n\ge 3\) satisfies the condition**.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Answer

The only admissible values are \(n=1\) and \(n=2\).

\[
\boxed{1+2=3}
\]

