        Let  

* \(n=64\) be the number of coins,  
* \(b_i\in\{0,1\}\;(i=1,\dots ,n)\) the state of the \(i^{\text{th}}\) coin  
 (\(b_i=1\) for **H**, \(b_i=0\) for **T**),  
* \(h(C)=\displaystyle\sum_{i=1}^{n}b_i\) the number of heads in a configuration \(C\),  
* \(S(C)=\displaystyle\sum_{i=1}^{n} i\,b_i\) the weighted sum of the positions of the heads.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  Finiteness of the game  

When the current configuration is \(C\) with \(h=h(C)>0\) we flip the
\(h^{\text{th}}\) coin.

*If the \(h^{\text{th}}\) coin is a head* (\(b_h=1\)):
\[
h\longrightarrow h-1,\qquad 
S\longrightarrow S-h .
\]

*If the \(h^{\text{th}}\) coin is a tail* (\(b_h=0\)):
\[
h\longrightarrow h+1,\qquad 
S\longrightarrow S+h .
\]

Define the quantity  

\[
P(C)=S(C)-\frac{h(C)\bigl(h(C)-1\bigr)}{2}.
\]

From the two cases above we obtain  

\[
\begin{array}{c|c|c}
\text{type of flip} & h' & S' \\ \hline
\text{head flipped} & h-1 & S-h\\
\text{tail flipped} & h+1 & S+h
\end{array}
\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad
\begin{array}{c|c}
\text{head flipped} & P' = P-1\\
\text{tail flipped} & P' = P .
\end{array}
\]

Thus **\(P\) never increases and it drops by exactly \(1\) whenever a head
is flipped**.  
Since \(P\) is a non‑negative integer, only finitely many head‑flips can
occur.  

If \(h>0\) then the smallest possible value of \(S\) is
\(1+2+\dots +h = h(h+1)/2\); consequently  

\[
P = S-\frac{h(h-1)}{2}\ge \frac{h(h+1)}{2}-\frac{h(h-1)}{2}=h>0 .
\]

Hence \(P=0\) can happen only when \(h=0\), i.e. when all coins show
tails.  Therefore the game must stop after a finite number of moves for
every initial configuration \(C\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  An explicit formula for \(\ell(C)\)

Let  

* \(H\) = number of head‑flips,
* \(T\) = number of tail‑flips,
* \(h_0=h(C)\) and \(S_0=S(C)\) for the initial configuration.

Each head‑flip reduces \(h\) by \(1\) and each tail‑flip increases \(h\) by
\(1\); starting from \(h_0\) and ending at \(0\) we have  

\[
H-T = h_0\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad T = H-h_0 .
\]

Because \(P\) drops by \(1\) at every head‑flip and ends at \(0\),

\[
H = P_0 = S_0-\frac{h_0(h_0-1)}{2}.
\]

Hence  

\[
\ell(C)=H+T = H+(H-h_0)=2H-h_0
        =2\Bigl(S_0-\frac{h_0(h_0-1)}{2}\Bigr)-h_0
        =2S_0-h_0^{2}.
\tag{1}
\]

So the number of turns needed from a configuration \(C\) is simply  

\[
\boxed{\;\ell(C)=2\sum_{i=1}^{n} i\,b_i-\Bigl(\sum_{i=1}^{n}b_i\Bigr)^{2}\; } .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Average value over all \(2^{64}\) configurations  

For a uniformly random configuration each coin is a head with probability
\(1/2\) independently.

* Expected number of heads  

\[
\mathbb{E}[h]=\frac{n}{2}.
\]

* Expected value of \(h^{2}\)  

\[
\mathbb{E}[h^{2}]
   =\operatorname{Var}(h)+\bigl(\mathbb{E}[h]\bigr)^{2}
   =\frac{n}{4}+\frac{n^{2}}{4}
   =\frac{n(n+1)}{4}.
\]

* Expected weighted sum  

\[
\mathbb{E}[S]=\sum_{i=1}^{n} i\;\mathbb{P}(b_i=1)
            =\frac12\sum_{i=1}^{n} i
            =\frac12\cdot\frac{n(n+1)}{2}
            =\frac{n(n+1)}{4}.
\]

Insert these expectations into (1):

\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbb{E}[\ell]
   &=2\,\mathbb{E}[S]-\mathbb{E}[h^{2}]   \\
   &=2\cdot\frac{n(n+1)}{4}-\frac{n(n+1)}{4}
     =\frac{n(n+1)}{4}.
\end{aligned}
\]

Thus for \(n=64\)

\[
\boxed{\displaystyle\mathbb{E}[\ell]=\frac{64\cdot65}{4}=16\cdot65=1040 } .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
**Answer.**  
The game always finishes after a finite number of moves; in fact for a
configuration \(C\) with heads at positions \(i\) the number of moves is
\(\ell(C)=2\sum i - (\#\text{heads})^{2}\).  Averaging over all
\(2^{64}\) initial configurations gives  

\[
\boxed{\,\text{average }\ell = 1040\, } .
\]

