--- [geometry.polygon.regular_polygon] ---
Triggers: regular 12-gon, rectangle counting, diagonal directions
Technique: Count axis-aligned rectangles in a regular 12-gon by classifying the 6 possible line directions (sides and diagonals) into perpendicular pairs, then counting valid rectangle configurations for each pair of perpendicular directions.
Question: Find the number of rectangles that can be formed inside a fixed regular dodecagon ( $12$ -gon) where each side of the rectangle lies on either a side or a diagonal of the dodecagon. The diagram below shows three of those rectangles. [asy] unitsize(0.6 inch); for(int i=0; i<360; i+=30) { dot(dir(i), 4+black); draw(dir(i)--dir(i+30)); } draw(dir(120)--dir(330)); filldraw(dir(210)--dir(240)--dir(30)--dir(60)--cycle, mediumgray, linewidth(1.5)); draw((0,0.366)--(0.366,0), linewidth(1.5)); [/asy]
Answer: A regular 12-gon has vertices at angles 30k degrees. Lines through sides and diagonals come in 6 directions (0, 30, 60, 90, 120, 150 degrees). A rectangle requires two pairs of parallel lines from two mutually perpendicular directions. The perpendicular direction pairs are (0,90), (30,120), and (60,150). For each perpendicular pair, enumerate all lines in those two directions that lie along sides or diagonals of the 12-gon, then count the number of ways to choose 2 lines from each direction to form a rectangle. Each direction has multiple parallel lines at different offsets; the count depends on how many parallel lines exist per direction in the 12-gon. Summing over all three perpendicular pairs and accounting for symmetry gives 315 rectangles.

