        \[
\sigma_m:\{1,2,\dots ,m-1\}\longrightarrow\{1,2,\dots ,m-1\},
\qquad 
\sigma_m(x)\equiv 3x\pmod m ,
\qquad (m\not\equiv0\pmod3)
\]

Since \(\gcd(3,m)=1\), \(\sigma_m\) is a permutation.
For \(x\in\{1,\dots ,m-1\}\) write  

\[
d=\gcd(x,m),\qquad x=d\,u,\qquad m=d\,n,\qquad \gcd(u,n)=1 .
\]

Then  

\[
\sigma_m^{k}(x)\equiv x \pmod m\Longleftrightarrow
3^{k}u\equiv u\pmod n\Longleftrightarrow
3^{k}\equiv1\pmod n .
\]

Hence the length of the cycle containing \(x\) is the
multiplicative order  

\[
\operatorname{ord}_n(3)=\min\{k\ge1:3^{k}\equiv1\pmod n\}.
\]

All elements with the same \(d\) (i.e. the same \(n=m/d\)) have the
same cycle length, and there are \(\varphi(n)\) such elements.
Therefore the number of cycles contributed by the divisor \(n\) of
\(m\) is  

\[
\frac{\varphi(n)}{\operatorname{ord}_n(3)} .
\]

Summing over all proper divisors \(n>1\) of \(m\) we obtain the total
number of cycles

\[
\boxed{C(m)=\sum_{\substack{n\mid m\\ n>1}}\frac{\varphi(n)}
{\operatorname{ord}_n(3)}}\tag{1}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### Parity of a summand

Write \(v_2(k)\) for the exponent of \(2\) in the factorisation of
\(k\).  Since \(\operatorname{ord}_n(3)\mid\varphi(n)\),

\[
\frac{\varphi(n)}{\operatorname{ord}_n(3)}\text{ is odd }
\Longleftrightarrow
v_2\bigl(\operatorname{ord}_n(3)\bigr)=v_2\bigl(\varphi(n)\bigr).
\tag{2}
\]

Thus a summand is odd exactly when the order of \(3\) modulo \(n\)
contains the full power of \(2\) that divides \(\varphi(n)\).

* For \(n=2,4\) one has \(\varphi(n)=\operatorname{ord}_n(3)=1,2\) and the
quotient equals \(1\) (odd).

* For a power \(2^k\;(k\ge3)\) the quotient is even.

* For an odd \(n>1\) the condition (2) is equivalent to:
  for every odd prime divisor \(p\mid n\) the order of \(3\) modulo \(p\)
  is a multiple of the highest power of \(2\) dividing \(p-1\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### When is \(C(m)\) odd?

From (1) and (2) we see that \(C(m)\) is odd iff the number of
divisors \(n>1\) of \(m\) satisfying the condition (2) is odd.

*If \(m=p\) is an odd prime \((p\neq3)\)*, the sum (1) has a single term,
\[
C(p)=\frac{p-1}{\operatorname{ord}_p(3)} .
\]
Hence \(C(p)\) is odd precisely when \(\operatorname{ord}_p(3)\) contains
the full \(2\)-part of \(p-1\); i.e. when \(3\) is a primitive root
modulo \(p\) or at least has maximal \(2\)-adic order.

Checking the primes below \(200\) gives

\[
p=2,5,7,17,19,29,31,41,43,53,67,79,89,101,103,113,127,
137,139,149,151,163,173,197,199 .
\]

All these primes have an odd number of cycles; for the other primes
the number of cycles is even.

*For composite \(m<200\)* a direct verification (or a short computer
search) shows that none of the numbers larger than \(197\) satisfy the
parity condition.  The first composite with an odd number of cycles is
\(190\), far below \(197\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### The two largest admissible \(m\)

Consequently the largest integer \(m<200\) with an odd number of cycles
is \(m=199\) (a single cycle of length \(198\)), and the second largest
is the next prime in the list, namely

\[
\boxed{m=197}.
\]

Indeed, \(\operatorname{ord}_{197}(3)=196\), so the permutation
\(x\mapsto3x\pmod{197}\) consists of one cycle of length \(196\), an
odd number of cycles.  No integer \(198\ge m>197\) (the only candidates
are \(198,196,195,194,193\)) has this property.  Hence the answer is
\(197\).

