### 1.  A necessary condition

Fix a flavour \(F\).  
If the distance (number of positions) between two consecutive occurrences of
\(F\) on the circle were larger than \(100\), then the block of length \(100\)
lying completely inside this gap would miss the flavour \(F\); this contradicts
the definition of a colourful word.  
Hence the distance between any two consecutive occurrences of a fixed flavour
is at most \(100\).

If a flavour occurs \(t\) times, the circle is split into \(t\) gaps whose
total length is \(m\); each gap is at most \(100\).  Consequently  

\[
m\le 100t\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad t\ge\Bigl\lceil\frac{m}{100}\Bigr\rceil .
\tag{1}
\]

Since there are 99 flavours, (1) gives the **necessary inequality**

\[
\boxed{m\ge 99\Bigl\lceil\frac{m}{100}\Bigr\rceil } .\tag{2}
\]

If (2) fails, the integer \(m\) cannot be colourful.

